"use strict";

// Paul Hoang, Business Management, 5th edition (IBID Press, 2022).
const TEXTBOOK = {
  "Business activity": {"d":[{"text":"Business activity is the process of turning inputs (land, labour, capital and enterprise) into outputs (finished goods and services) in order to meet the needs and wants of different customers.","pages":[19]}]},
  "Needs and wants": {"d":[{"text":"Needs are the basic necessities that a person must have to survive, including food, water, warmth, shelter and clothing.","pages":[20]},{"text":"Wants are people's desires, i.e. the things they would like to have, such as new clothes, smartphones, overseas holidays and jewellery.","pages":[20]}]},
  "Goods and services": {"d":[{"text":"Goods are physical products produced and sold to customers, such as laptops, books, contact lenses, perfumes and children's toys.","pages":[20]},{"text":"Services are intangible products sold to customers, such as the services provided by airlines, restaurants, cinemas, banks, health and beauty spas, schools and hospitals.","pages":[20]}]},
  "Customers and consumers": {"d":[{"text":"Customers are the people or organizations that buy the product.","pages":[20]},{"text":"Consumers are the people or organizations that actually use a product.","pages":[20]}]},
  "Factors of production": {"d":[{"text":"The factors of production are the resources used in the production process.","pages":[477]},{"text":"Essentially, the role of production is to turn factors of production (comprised of land, labour and capital resources as well as entrepreneurship) into the output of goods and services in a cost-effective way.","pages":[477]}],"kind":"Textbook passage"},
  "Added value": {"d":[{"text":"Adding value is the practice of producing a good or service that is worth more than the cost of the resources used in the production process.","pages":[20]}]},
  "Business functions": {"d":[{"text":"For a business to operate effectively, tasks must be carried out by various functional areas (or departments). These interdependent functional areas are human resources, finance and accounts, marketing and operations management. The nature of business requires these main functional areas to work together in order to achieve the organization's goals.","pages":[12]}],"kind":"Textbook passage"},
  "Primary sector": {"d":[{"text":"Primary sector refers to businesses involved in the cultivation or extraction of natural resources, such as farming, mining, quarrying, fishing, oil exploration and forestry.","pages":[20]}]},
  "Secondary sector": {"d":[{"text":"Secondary sector refers to businesses concerned with the construction and manufacturing of products.","pages":[20]}]},
  "Tertiary sector": {"d":[{"text":"Tertiary sector refers to businesses involved with the provision of services to customers.","pages":[20]}]},
  "Quaternary sector": {"d":[{"text":"Quaternary sector is a sub-category of the tertiary sector, where businesses are involved in intellectual and knowledge-based activities that generate and share information, such as research organizations.","pages":[20]}]},
  "Chain of production": {"d":[{"text":"The four business sectors are linked through the chain of production which tracks the stages of an item's production, from the extraction of raw materials used to make the product all the way through to it being delivered to the consumer (see Figure 1.1).","pages":[15]}]},
  "Entrepreneurship": {"d":[{"text":"Entrepreneurship refers to the collective knowledge, skills and experiences of entrepreneurs.","pages":[20]}]},
  "Start-up opportunities and challenges": {"d":[{"text":"In summary, people set up their own businesses due to the opportunities to satisfy their personal desires - such as to fulfil a personal vision, to have the opportunity to achieve success, to be their own boss or simply to live a more extravagant lifestyle (if and when the business becomes a 'success'). However, a significant number of new businesses fail to survive. There are three inter-related reasons or challenges behind this: a lack of cash in the business, poor cost control and substandard or weak management and leadership.","pages":[18,19]}],"kind":"Textbook passage","lists":[{"heading":"Opportunities for starting a business","items":[{"text":"Entrepreneurs tend to benefit personally when there is an appreciation in the value of their businesses, especially as property and land tend to increase in value over time. This is called capital growth.","pages":[18],"point":"Growth"},{"text":"The Chinese have a saying that \"You can never get rich earning money from working for someone else.\" That is because the potential returns from setting up your own business can easily outweigh the costs, even though the risks are high.","pages":[18],"point":"Earnings"},{"text":"In many societies, it is the cultural norm to pass on assets, including businesses, to the next generation. Many self-employed entrepreneurs view their business as something that they are able to pass on (transference) to their children (inheritance) to give them a sense of financial security that might not be possible if they chose to work for someone else.","pages":[18],"point":"Transference and inheritance"},{"text":"Some people might view setting up and running a business as a personal challenge. It is this challenge that drives them to perform and what gives them particular satisfaction.","pages":[18],"point":"Challenge"},{"text":"Employees have to follow the instructions and rules set by the organization that they work for, such as the conditions of employment, working hours, employment benefits and holiday entitlement. Conversely, being self-employed means that there is autonomy (independence, freedom of choice and flexibility) in how things are done within the organization.","pages":[18],"point":"Autonomy"},{"text":"Similarly, there could be a greater sense of job security for someone who is their own boss. By contrast, employees can be dismissed, made redundant or even replaced by technology.","pages":[18],"point":"Security"},{"text":"Some people might want to pursue their passion or to turn their hobby and interests into a business opportunity. Successful entrepreneurs have a passion for what they do and this is made easier if the nature of the work is directly related to their personal interests.","pages":[18],"point":"Hobbies"}]},{"heading":"Challenges for starting a business","items":[{"text":"All businesses need finance for the purchase of fixed assets, such as premises, buildings, machinery and equipment. However, start-up firms and most owners of new or small businesses do not have the credentials to secure sufficient funding without major challenges.","pages":[16],"point":"Lack of finance"},{"text":"A major challenge facing new businesses is attracting customers, i.e. building a broad and loyal customer base. The problem is intensified when there are well established competitors that already operate in the market.","pages":[16],"point":"Unestablished customer base"},{"text":"Financing working capital (the money available for the daily running of a business) is a major challenge for many business start-ups. A business might have a lot of stock, such as raw materials, semi-finished output or finished goods that it cannot easily turn into cash.","pages":[16],"point":"Cash flow problems"},{"text":"Marketing challenges arise when businesses fail to meet customer needs, thereby resulting in poor sales and a lack of profitability. Supplying the right products to the right customers at the right price is especially crucial for new businesses.","pages":[16],"point":"Marketing problems"},{"text":"Business start-ups may lack experience in hiring the right staff with all the necessary skills. This can lead to poor levels of labour productivity and the need to retrain staff or to rehire people, all of which can be very expensive and time consuming.","pages":[17],"point":"People management problems"},{"text":"It can be challenging for business start-ups to accurately forecast levels of demand so they are more likely to either over produce or under produce. Overproduction tends to lead to stockpiling, wastage and increased costs (see Chapter 40).","pages":[17],"point":"Production problems"},{"text":"It is necessary for businesses to comply with all necessary legislation, including business registration procedures, insurance cover for staff and buildings, consumer protection laws and rules about intellectual property such as copyrights, patents and trademarks (see Chapter 42). The paperwork and legal requirements of setting up a new business can be cumbersome, confusing, time consuming and expensive.","pages":[17],"point":"Legalities"},{"text":"New businesses are likely to have high set-up costs (see Box 1.2) and running costs due to the large amount of money needed to purchase or pay for capital equipment, machinery, stocks (inventory), rent, advertising, insurance and so forth. Smaller businesses will also be at a cost disadvantage as they cannot benefit from economies of scale (see Chapter 5).","pages":[17],"point":"High production costs"},{"text":"Businesses face a dilemma in the location decision: busy areas offer the highest potential number of customers, but the premises in these areas will also cost the most (see Chapter 38). Fixed costs, such as rent or mortgage payments, account for a large percentage of total costs for many businesses.","pages":[17],"point":"Poor location"},{"text":"All businesses, irrespective of size or how long they have been in operation, are prone to exogenous shocks (see Chapter 46) that create a challenging trading environment, such as a global financial crisis or the outbreak of a pandemic. However, larger and more established firms tend to be better resourced to handle these external influences.","pages":[17],"point":"External influences"}]}]},
  "Business plan": {"d":[{"text":"Business plan refers to the document that sets out a business idea or proposition, including the objectives, resources (marketing, operations, personnel and finance) and corporate strategies.","pages":[644]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages of business plans","items":[{"text":"Business plans are important as a management planning tool as they form a strategic focus. In particular, start-ups and small businesses need to focus on their unique identities, target markets and their goods or services tailored to match the needs and wants of the market.","pages":[643]},{"text":"Furthermore, a business plan provides a framework or structure as a planning tool to keep the business focused on its goals and objectives. Used appropriately and referred to regularly, a business plan helps decision makers and entrepreneurs to manage their priorities and different areas of focus.","pages":[643]},{"text":"Business plans can also be important for managing change in business organizations. With good planning, decision makers and entrepreneurs regularly review key performance indicators (such as sales growth or market share data), track progress and pursue new business opportunities.","pages":[643]},{"text":"The business plan also helps to reassure financial lenders, such as commercial banks and business angels (see Chapter 15), that the entrepreneur has comprehensively researched the business idea and has given reasons to support their proposal.","pages":[641]},{"text":"Hence, it is common for a new business to include a cash flow forecast in the business plan so that provisions can be made to cover any shortfalls.","pages":[643]},{"text":"Nevertheless, business plans as a planning tool ultimately allows organizations to see the bigger picture. They can include additional business management tools such as SWOT analysis (see Chapter 44), STEEPLE analysis (see Chapter 46) and aspects of Porter's generic strategies (see Chapter 53).","pages":[644]}]},{"heading":"Limitations of business plans","items":[{"text":"However, to produce an appropriate business plan is likely to be both time consuming and costly. For example, there is a need to undertake sufficient market research (see Chapter 26), as well as making detailed plans for the different functional areas of the business (operations management, marketing, human resources and finance) and contingency plans (see Chapter 41).","pages":[643]},{"text":"Furthermore, no amount of forward planning can eliminate risks. For example, new businesses that were set up in 2019 would not have planned for the global recession caused by the outbreak of the COVID-19 virus which caused a worldwide pandemic.","pages":[644]},{"text":"Although business plans are commonly used by new start-ups, quite often the owners and entrepreneurs of these businesses have limited experience. Hence, their business plans may lack depth and breadth of data and information.","pages":[644]},{"text":"Even with some experience, the usefulness of business plans will depend on how accurate the forecasts are (such as sales forecasts and cost structures) as well as whether the right strategies are implemented effectively in a continually changing business world. Simply having a business plan does not guarantee success no matter how comprehensive and detailed the plan might be.","pages":[644]}]}]},
  "Private sector": {"d":[{"text":"The private sector is the part of the economy run by private individuals and businesses, rather than by the government, such as sole traders, partnerships, privately held companies and publicly held companies.","pages":[37]}]},
  "Public sector": {"d":[{"text":"The public sector is the part of the economy controlled by the government. Examples include state healthcare and education services, the emergency services, social housing and national defence.","pages":[38]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Reasons for public sector business activity","items":[{"text":"To ensure that everyone has access to basic services such as education, health care, museums, public parks and public libraries.","pages":[22]},{"text":"To avoid wasteful competition as the government is able to achieve huge economies of scale (cost savings from operating on a large magnitude) in the provision of certain services, such as postal services and national defence.","pages":[22]},{"text":"To protect citizens and businesses through institutions such as the Police and the courts that govern the country's law and order system.","pages":[22]},{"text":"To create employment opportunities, e.g. governments tend to be a large employer of teachers, doctors and nurses.","pages":[22]},{"text":"To stabilise the economy, e.g. many private sector commercial banks were nationalised (bought by the government) during the global credit crisis of 2008 to prevent further financial turmoil. Government intervention throughout the COVID-19 global pandemic also helped to stabilise the world's economies.","pages":[22]}]}]},
  "Limited liability": {"d":[{"text":"Limited liability is a restriction on the amount of money that owners of a company can lose if the business goes bankrupt, i.e. shareholders cannot lose more than the amount they invested in the company.","pages":[37]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantage for the company","items":[{"text":"As all companies have limited liability, it is easier to attract investors as the risks are relatively low for them.","pages":[28],"point":"Limited liability"}]}]},
  "Unlimited liability": {"d":[{"text":"Unlimited liability is a feature of sole traders and ordinary partnerships who are legally liable or responsible for all monies owed to their creditors, even if this means that they have to sell their personal possessions to pay for their debts.","pages":[38]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Purpose of unlimited liability","items":[{"text":"Unlimited liability exists to prevent sole traders and partners from making careless and irresponsible decisions in managing their businesses. It makes private individuals accountable for their actions and decisions.","pages":[26]}]},{"heading":"Risk for owners","items":[{"text":"However, the risk of loss of private property and personal possessions can deter sole traders and partners from taking risks, choosing to make safe decisions instead.","pages":[26]}]}]},
  "Incorporation": {"d":[{"text":"Incorporation means that there is a legal difference between the owners of a company and the business itself. This ensures that the owners are protected by limited liability.","pages":[37]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages of incorporation","items":[{"text":"As all companies have limited liability, it is easier to attract investors as the risks are relatively low for them.","pages":[28],"point":"Limited liability"},{"text":"Unlike partnerships and sole traders, the legal difference between the company and its owners means it can continue to operate as a separate legal entity, even with a change of owners.","pages":[28],"point":"Continuity"}]},{"heading":"Requirements for establishing a company","items":[{"text":"Setting up a company can be complicated and expensive, e.g. there are rules and regulations that must be obeyed for shares to be sold on the stock exchange.","pages":[26]}]}]},
  "Sole trader": {"d":[{"text":"A sole trader is a self-employed person who runs the business on his/her own. This mean s/he has exclusive responsibility for its success (profits) or failure (unlimited liability).","pages":[38]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages of sole proprietorships","items":[{"text":"Sole traders are quite easy to set up and start-up costs are usually much lower in comparison to starting other types of business entities.","pages":[24],"point":"Few legal formalities"},{"text":"The sole trader is the only owner and therefore receives all of any profits earned by the business. This gives the sole trader an incentive to work hard and to become successful.","pages":[24],"point":"Profit taking"},{"text":"Sole traders do not take orders from anyone, have flexibility in decision-making (such as dictating their own working hours) and self-esteem from being successful.","pages":[24],"point":"Being your own boss"},{"text":"Sole traders can provide a personalised service to customers. Larger businesses might not have the time to get to know all their customers, so their services often become generic and impersonal.","pages":[24],"point":"Personalised service"},{"text":"Unlike other types of business entities, sole traders enjoy privacy as they do not have to make their financial records available to the general public, i.e. the owner enjoys confidentiality (although the accounts can be scrutinised by the relevant tax authorities).","pages":[24],"point":"Privacy"},{"text":"There are no other owners to consult, so sole traders can make decisions quickly and independently based on their own needs and preferences.","pages":[24],"point":"Quicker decision-making"}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages of sole proprietorships","items":[{"text":"As an unincorporated business, there is no limit to the amount of debts that a sole trader is legally responsible for if the business fails.","pages":[24],"point":"Unlimited liability"},{"text":"Sole traders often find it difficult to secure funds beyond personal savings. Growth can also be problematic due to the lack of sources of finance available to sole traders.","pages":[24],"point":"Limited sources of finance"},{"text":"Statistically, sole proprietorships have the largest risk of failure. The presence of larger and more established firms creates a huge threat to the profits and survival of smaller businesses.","pages":[24],"point":"High risks"},{"text":"Owners often have to do their own accounts, marketing and human resource management. The sole trader is unlikely to be equally effective in these different roles, so there is added workload and stress.","pages":[24],"point":"Workload and stress"},{"text":"A sole trader is not able to exploit the benefits of large-scale production, so their prices might be less competitive compared with those of larger rivals. This tends to reduce the competitiveness and profits for the sole proprietor.","pages":[24],"point":"Limited economies of scale"},{"text":"The running of the business can be jeopardised if the owner is not present. If the owner decides to go on holiday or becomes ill, the business might have problems in continuing.","pages":[24],"point":"Lack of continuity"}]}]},
  "Partnership": {"d":[{"text":"Partnerships are a type of private sector business entity owned by 2-20 people (known as partners). They share the responsibilities and burdens of running and owning the business.","pages":[37]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages of partnerships","items":[{"text":"Partnerships have more financial strength than sole proprietorships as there are more owners who can invest in the business, yet they are still fairly easy to set up. In general, it is also easier for partnerships to secure external sources of finance due to the lower risks.","pages":[25],"point":"Financial strength"},{"text":"Unlike sole traders, partners can benefit from shared expertise, shared workload and moral support. For example, a law firm might have partners who specialise in corporate law, divorce law and criminal law.","pages":[25],"point":"Specialisation and division of labour"},{"text":"Like sole proprietorships, partnerships do not have to publicise their financial records. Therefore, they can enjoy a fair degree of financial privacy.","pages":[25],"point":"Financial privacy"},{"text":"Partnerships can be more cost-effective than sole traders as each partner specializes in certain aspects of their business, thus raising labour productivity and operational efficiency.","pages":[25],"point":"Cost-effectiveness"}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages of partnerships","items":[{"text":"Legally, partnerships are responsible for their debts 'wholly or severally' meaning the debts can be repaid by either one partner (wholly) or shared among the partners (severally). The rare exception is with limited liability partners who have been elected to have limited liability.","pages":[25],"point":"Unlimited liability"},{"text":"Problems may exist if a partner leaves the firm or passes away because the partnership deed becomes invalid, i.e. it has to be set up again. It is possible to accommodate some changes in a deed of partnership, although solicitors will need to spend time (and money) on drafting the new contract.","pages":[25],"point":"A lack of continuity"},{"text":"In comparison to sole traders, decision-making is likely to take longer as there are more owners involved. Disagreements and conflict might also occur.","pages":[25],"point":"Prolonged decision-making"},{"text":"Disagreements and conflict within partnerships is common, but there must also be mutual trust. Each partner is legally and financially accountable to all others, so a mistake made by one person can reduce the profits for every partner.","pages":[25],"point":"Lack of harmony"}]}]},
  "Privately held company": {"d":[{"text":"A privately held company is a business owned by shareholders with limited liability but whose shares cannot be bought by or sold to the general public on a Stock Exchange.","pages":[37]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages of limited liability companies","items":[{"text":"Companies can raise large amounts of capital by selling shares. There are no interest charges and shareholders are paid dividends only if the company earns a profit.","pages":[28],"point":"Raising finance"},{"text":"As all companies have limited liability, it is easier to attract investors as the risks are relatively low for them.","pages":[28],"point":"Limited liability"},{"text":"Unlike partnerships and sole traders, the legal difference between the company and its owners means it can continue to operate as a separate legal entity, even with a change of owners.","pages":[28],"point":"Continuity"},{"text":"Due to their larger size, companies can benefit from economies of scale (lower unit costs of production as the firms grow). For example, it is usually cheaper for a company to borrow money than it is for sole traders or partnerships as limited companies are less of a financial risk.","pages":[28],"point":"Economies of scale"},{"text":"Companies can hire directors and specialist managers to run the firm as there is no need for the owners to be directly involved in the daily running of the business. Therefore, the output and productivity levels of limited liability companies are generally higher than found in sole proprietorships and partnerships.","pages":[28],"point":"Productivity"},{"text":"The highest income tax rate tends to be greater than the rate for corporation tax. Companies also benefit from a wider range of allowances and tax-deductible costs.","pages":[28],"point":"Tax benefits"}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages of limited liability companies","items":[{"text":"Quite often, as a company becomes larger, services and relationships can become more impersonal to both customers and employees.","pages":[28],"point":"Communication problems"},{"text":"Running a sole-proprietorship or partnership is cheaper and less bureaucratic than running a limited liability company.","pages":[28],"point":"Added complexities"},{"text":"Complying with the rules and regulations of being a company add to its running costs. This is particularly the case for publicly held companies due to stock exchange rules and regulations.","pages":[28],"point":"Compliance costs"},{"text":"Financial data must be provided to all shareholders. This can be a time consuming and expensive task as auditors have to be hired and Annual Reports have to be published and distributed.","pages":[28],"point":"Disclosure of information"},{"text":"There is far more bureaucracy involved in setting up and running a company, e.g. in the UK, the Companies Act 2006 states that a minimum of £50,000 ($70,000) must be issued as share capital. Solicitors must be hired to ensure that all documents are legally accurate.","pages":[28],"point":"Bureaucracy"}]}]},
  "Publicly held company": {"d":[{"text":"A publicly held company is an incorporated limited liability business that allows shareholders to buy and sell shares in the company via a public Stock Exchange.","pages":[38]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages of limited liability companies","items":[{"text":"Companies can raise large amounts of capital by selling shares. There are no interest charges and shareholders are paid dividends only if the company earns a profit.","pages":[28],"point":"Raising finance"},{"text":"As all companies have limited liability, it is easier to attract investors as the risks are relatively low for them.","pages":[28],"point":"Limited liability"},{"text":"Unlike partnerships and sole traders, the legal difference between the company and its owners means it can continue to operate as a separate legal entity, even with a change of owners.","pages":[28],"point":"Continuity"},{"text":"Due to their larger size, companies can benefit from economies of scale (lower unit costs of production as the firms grow). For example, it is usually cheaper for a company to borrow money than it is for sole traders or partnerships as limited companies are less of a financial risk.","pages":[28],"point":"Economies of scale"},{"text":"Companies can hire directors and specialist managers to run the firm as there is no need for the owners to be directly involved in the daily running of the business. Therefore, the output and productivity levels of limited liability companies are generally higher than found in sole proprietorships and partnerships.","pages":[28],"point":"Productivity"},{"text":"The highest income tax rate tends to be greater than the rate for corporation tax. Companies also benefit from a wider range of allowances and tax-deductible costs.","pages":[28],"point":"Tax benefits"}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages of limited liability companies","items":[{"text":"Quite often, as a company becomes larger, services and relationships can become more impersonal to both customers and employees.","pages":[28],"point":"Communication problems"},{"text":"Running a sole-proprietorship or partnership is cheaper and less bureaucratic than running a limited liability company.","pages":[28],"point":"Added complexities"},{"text":"Complying with the rules and regulations of being a company add to its running costs. This is particularly the case for publicly held companies due to stock exchange rules and regulations.","pages":[28],"point":"Compliance costs"},{"text":"Financial data must be provided to all shareholders. This can be a time consuming and expensive task as auditors have to be hired and Annual Reports have to be published and distributed.","pages":[28],"point":"Disclosure of information"},{"text":"There is far more bureaucracy involved in setting up and running a company, e.g. in the UK, the Companies Act 2006 states that a minimum of £50,000 ($70,000) must be issued as share capital. Solicitors must be hired to ensure that all documents are legally accurate.","pages":[28],"point":"Bureaucracy"},{"text":"Whilst sole traders and partnerships retain control of their businesses, publicly held companies face the potential threat of a takeover by a rival company that purchases a majority stake in the business (as shares are openly available for purchase on a public stock exchange).","pages":[28],"point":"Loss of control"}]}]},
  "Initial public offering (IPO)": {"d":[{"text":"An initial public offering (IPO) occurs when a business sells all or part of its business to shareholders on a public stock exchange for the first time. This changes the legal status of the business to a publicly held company.","pages":[37]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantage of flotation","items":[{"text":"Flotation helps to generate additional sources of finance for the company.","pages":[29]}]},{"heading":"Costs and risks","items":[{"text":"Advertising and promoting the company's initial public offering (IPO) adds to the costs.","pages":[28]},{"text":"Whilst sole traders and partnerships retain control of their businesses, publicly held companies face the potential threat of a takeover by a rival company that purchases a majority stake in the business (as shares are openly available for purchase on a public stock exchange).","pages":[28],"point":"Loss of control"}]}]},
  "For-profit social enterprise": {"d":[{"text":"Social enterprises are revenue-generating businesses with social objectives at the core of their operations. They can be for-profit or non-profit business entities, but all profits or surpluses must be reinvested for that social purpose rather than being distributed to shareholders and owners.","pages":[38]},{"text":"Whilst commercial for-profit businesses strive to return a profit for their owners, for-profit social enterprises strive to return a surplus for social gain rather than personal gain.","pages":[31]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Benefits of social enterprises","items":[{"text":"They use any financial surplus to benefit others in society, beyond personal rewards for shareholders and owners.","pages":[31]},{"text":"They create employment opportunities, thereby improving the economic and social landscape of local communities.","pages":[31]},{"text":"They are run in a transparent way, providing tangible benefits in addition to having a clean corporate conscience in the strive to achieve its social mission.","pages":[31]}]}]},
  "Private-sector social enterprise": {"d":[{"text":"Private sector for-profit social enterprises operate in a similar way to traditional for-profit businesses, but it is what these organizations do with their profits or financial surplus that sets them apart. For-profit social enterprises reinvest or donate any surplus to create positive social change. Furthermore, private sector for-profit social enterprises use ethical business practises to achieve their social aims related to the needs of local communities and societies.","pages":[31]}]},
  "Public-sector social enterprise": {"d":[{"text":"Public sector for-profit social enterprises are state-owned enterprises run in a commercial way. They are formed by the government through legal means and regulated as they participate in commercial business activities for financial gain.","pages":[32]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Benefit of public sector companies","items":[{"text":"They help to raise much-needed government revenues yet provide essential services that may be inefficient and undesirable if left solely to the private sector.","pages":[32]}]}]},
  "Cooperative": {"d":[{"text":"Cooperatives are for-profit social enterprises set up, owned and run by their members, who might be employees and/or customers.","pages":[37]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages of cooperatives","items":[{"text":"Employees have a stake in the cooperative so are more interested in how it performs. This can enhance staff motivation and productivity.","pages":[34],"point":"Incentives to work"},{"text":"Employees have a say in how the business is run. There is a democratic system of members having equal voting rights.","pages":[34],"point":"Decision-making power"},{"text":"Cooperatives are run on socially responsible principles leading to gains for other members of society rather than only for the owners. Thus, cooperatives create social gains that can be enjoyed by the wider community.","pages":[34],"point":"Social benefits"},{"text":"A key advantage of social enterprises such as cooperatives is that there tends to be public support, i.e. people (including customers) want to help them succeed because they believe in the cause the cooperative stands for.","pages":[34],"point":"Public support"}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages of cooperatives","items":[{"text":"There might be ineffective managers and employees as cooperatives do not pay high salaries and bonuses as incentives to work.","pages":[34],"point":"Disincentive effects"},{"text":"Cooperatives might suffer from a lack of finance as most of them cannot raise funds through a stock exchange. Sources of finance are often limited to the amount contributed by their members.","pages":[34],"point":"Limited sources of finance"},{"text":"Decisions are likely to be slowed down (delayed) as all members of the cooperative work in a democratic way and are involved in the decision-making process. Every member has a voice in the organization.","pages":[34],"point":"Slower decision-making"},{"text":"Cooperatives tend to have flatter organizational structures (see Chapter 8), so there are fewer opportunities for employees to progress in their professional careers.","pages":[34],"point":"Limited promotional opportunities"}]}]},
  "Non-governmental organization (NGO)": {"d":[{"text":"Non-governmental organizations (NGOs) are private sector not-for-profit social enterprises that operate for the benefit of others rather than primarily aiming to earn a profit, such as Oxfam and Friends of the Earth.","pages":[37]}]},
  "Vision statement": {"d":[{"text":"A vision statement is an organization's long-term aspirations, i.e. where the business ultimately wants to be.","pages":[52]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Purpose of a vision statement","items":[{"text":"Vision statements typically relate to the attainment of success, i.e. visualisation of what success would look like.","pages":[39]},{"text":"Instead, vision statements allow people to see what could be.","pages":[40]}]},{"heading":"Limitations of vision and mission statements","items":[{"text":"Some critics argue that such statements are no more than a public relations stunt. After all, the ultimate purpose of most businesses, they argue, is to maximize profits and protect shareholder value.","pages":[40]},{"text":"Constructing meaningful vision and mission statements can also be very time consuming; it is difficult to draft a statement that caters for all the dynamics of a business and meets the needs of every stakeholder group.","pages":[40]},{"text":"Even the best thought-out statements might not be supported by everyone, such as employees on part-time or temporary contracts. In such cases, it can be a lengthy process to convert people's beliefs and behaviour.","pages":[40]}]}]},
  "Mission statement": {"d":[{"text":"A mission statement refers to the declaration of an organization's overall purpose. It forms the foundation for setting the objectives of a business.","pages":[52]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Purpose of a mission statement","items":[{"text":"It provides a sense of direction, guides decision-making and unifies all people and corporate cultures within the organization in an attempt to achieve the overall vision.","pages":[39]},{"text":"The mission statement tends to outline the values of the business, i.e. its beliefs and guiding principles that set the framework for how managers and employees operate on a daily basis.","pages":[40]}]},{"heading":"Limitations of vision and mission statements","items":[{"text":"Some critics argue that such statements are no more than a public relations stunt. After all, the ultimate purpose of most businesses, they argue, is to maximize profits and protect shareholder value.","pages":[40]},{"text":"Constructing meaningful vision and mission statements can also be very time consuming; it is difficult to draft a statement that caters for all the dynamics of a business and meets the needs of every stakeholder group.","pages":[40]},{"text":"Even the best thought-out statements might not be supported by everyone, such as employees on part-time or temporary contracts. In such cases, it can be a lengthy process to convert people's beliefs and behaviour.","pages":[40]}]}]},
  "Strategic objectives": {"d":[{"text":"Strategic objectives are the longer-term goals of a business, such as profit maximization, growth, market standing and increased market share.","pages":[52]}]},
  "Tactical objectives": {"d":[{"text":"Tactical objectives are short-term goals that affect a unit of the organization. They are specific goals that guide the daily functioning of certain departments or operations.","pages":[52]}]},
  "Strategy and tactics": {"d":[{"text":"Strategies are the various plans of action that businesses use to achieve their targets. They are the long-term plans of the organization as a whole.","pages":[52]},{"text":"Tactics are the short-term plans of action that businesses use to achieve their objectives.","pages":[52]}]},
  "SMART objectives": {"d":[{"text":"Marketing objectives that are SMART (specific, measurable, agreed, realistic and time bound), such as the expected market share or sales turnover within a specified time frame.","pages":[353]}],"kind":"Textbook passage","lists":[{"heading":"Reasons for setting organizational objectives","items":[{"text":"Business objectives help to control a firm's plans as they determine the parameters for business activity. Essentially, objectives provide the basis for measuring and controlling the performance of the business as a whole.","pages":[41],"point":"To measure and control"},{"text":"Business objectives can help to inspire managers and employees to reach a common goal, thus helping to unify and motivate the workforce. They also encourage managers to think strategically, i.e. to plan for the long term success of the business.","pages":[41],"point":"To motivate"},{"text":"Business objectives provide an agreed and clear focus (or sense of purpose) for all individuals and departments of an organization. As the foundation for decision-making, organizational objectives are used to devise appropriate business strategies.","pages":[41],"point":"To direct"}]}]},
  "Growth objective": {"d":[{"text":"The growth of a business is usually measured by an increase in its sales revenues or by its market share (the firm's sales revenue expressed as a percentage of the industry's total sales revenue).","pages":[42]}],"kind":"Textbook passage","lists":[{"heading":"Importance of growth","items":[{"text":"Growth is essential for the survival of a business in an ever-changing and competitive world, especially with the exposure of businesses to mergers and takeovers (see Chapter 6). The failure to grow may result in declining competitiveness and threaten the firm's sustainability.","pages":[42]}]}]},
  "Profit objective": {"d":[{"text":"Traditionally, the main business objective of most private sector organizations is to maximize profits.","pages":[42]}],"kind":"Textbook passage","lists":[{"heading":"Role of profit","items":[{"text":"Profit provides an incentive for entrepreneurs to take risks in setting up and running a business.","pages":[42]},{"text":"For incorporated businesses (Chapter 2), a proportion of the profits (called dividends) is distributed to their shareholders.","pages":[42]}]}]},
  "Protecting shareholder value": {"d":[{"text":"This business objective is about generating long-term value for shareholders (owners) of the business. Protecting and maximizing shareholder value is about earning a profitable return for shareholders in a sustainable way.","pages":[42]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Challenge for the directors","items":[{"text":"A challenge for the directors is to balance short-term profits (in the form of dividends) with an investment in the long-term value of the company.","pages":[42]}]}]},
  "Ethical objectives": {"d":[{"text":"Ethical objectives are organizational goals based on moral guidelines, determined by the business and/or society, which direct and determine decision-making.","pages":[52]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages of ethical objectives and practices","items":[{"text":"Acting ethically and in a socially responsible way can help to enhance the corporate image and reputation of a business. Conversely, the media will report unethical business behaviour which could seriously damage the firm's corporate image.","pages":[44],"point":"Improved corporate image"},{"text":"Customers are more likely to be loyal to a business that does not act immorally. For example, The Body Shop has established a large customer base worldwide based on its ethical policy of not testing its products on animals.","pages":[44],"point":"Increased customer loyalty"},{"text":"Ethical behaviour can help to cut certain costs, e.g. being environmentally friendly can reduce the amount of (excess) packaging. Ethical objectives and strategies can help the firm to avoid litigation costs (expenses associated with legal action taken against a business) that might otherwise arise from unethical and irresponsible activities.","pages":[44],"point":"Cost cutting"},{"text":"Ethical behaviour can help a business to attract and retain highly motivated staff. People are more likely to be proud of the firm they work for if it acts ethically and within the law.","pages":[44],"point":"Improved staff morale and motivation"}]},{"heading":"Limitations of ethical objectives and practices","items":[{"text":"The costs of being socially responsible are potentially very high. For example, producing organic agricultural products is far more expensive than growing and harvesting genetically modified crops due to the additional time and money involved.","pages":[44],"point":"Compliance costs"},{"text":"If compliance costs cannot be passed onto consumers in the form of higher prices, the firm's profitability is likely to fall. An ethical dilemma for the business exists when ethical decision-making involves adopting a less profitable course of action.","pages":[44],"point":"Lower profits"},{"text":"Not all stakeholders are keen on the firm adopting ethical objectives, especially if this conflicts with other business objectives such as profit maximization. Speculative shareholders and investors may be more interested in short-term profits than the firm's long-term ethical stance.","pages":[44],"point":"Stakeholder conflict"},{"text":"Views about what is considered right or wrong depend on the beliefs and principles held by individuals and societies. Legislation can help to provide guidelines about what is socially acceptable, but even these are somewhat subjective in nature.","pages":[44],"point":"The subjective nature of business ethics"}]}]},
  "Ethical code of practice": {"d":[{"text":"An ethical code of practice is the documented beliefs and philosophies of an organization, so that people know what is considered acceptable or not acceptable within the organization.","pages":[52]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Purpose of an ethical code of practice","items":[{"text":"It is important because people need to know what is considered acceptable or not acceptable within an organization, such as guidelines and expectations on employee behaviour.","pages":[43]},{"text":"Hence, a code of ethics, such as having personal integrity and consideration for others, can provide a framework for consistency and uniformity in the workplace.","pages":[43]}]}]},
  "Survival objective": {"d":[{"text":"Survival - New and unestablished businesses are likely to encounter a number of problems (see Chapter 2) such as a lack of brand recognition, a small customer base and/or intense rivalry from existing firms. Hence, survival becomes a key tactical objective.","pages":[45]}],"kind":"Textbook passage"},
  "Corporate social responsibility (CSR)": {"d":[{"text":"Corporate social responsibility (CSR) is the conscientious consideration of ethical and environmental practice related to business activity. A business that adopts CSR acts morally towards all of its various stakeholder groups and the well-being of society as a whole.","pages":[52]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantage of corporate social responsibility","items":[{"text":"Businesses have long realised that a good reputation (how others view the organization) can give them an important competitive advantage.","pages":[48]}]},{"heading":"Limitations of corporate social responsibility","items":[{"text":"what is considered 'right' or 'wrong' is largely based on public opinion. The subjective nature of CSR also means that attitudes may change over time.","pages":[48],"point":"The nature of CSR is rather subjective"},{"text":"CSR is further complicated when businesses operate in different countries. This is because what might be considered acceptable in one country might be totally undesirable in others.","pages":[48]},{"text":"Acting in a socially responsible way can help to improve a firm's reputation, but the compliance costs will add to its expenses.","pages":[48]},{"text":"For employees and investors to support the organization to meet its ethical objectives, they must be convinced that CSR is in their best interest too.","pages":[48]}]}]},
  "Stakeholders": {"d":[{"text":"Stakeholders are individuals or organizations with a direct interest (known as a stake) in the activities and performance of a business, such as shareholders, employees, customers and suppliers.","pages":[64]}]},
  "Internal stakeholders": {"d":[{"text":"Internal stakeholders of a business are members of the organization, namely the employees, managers, directors and shareholders (owners) of the business.","pages":[63]}]},
  "External stakeholders": {"d":[{"text":"External stakeholders are individuals and organizations not part of the business but have a direct interest in its activities and performance. Examples include customers, suppliers and the government.","pages":[63]}]},
  "Owners and shareholders": {"d":[{"text":"Shareholders (or stockholders) are the owners of a limited liability company. Shares in a company can be held by individuals and other organizations.","pages":[64]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Shareholders’ objectives","items":[{"text":"To maximise dividends (a proportion of the company's profits distributed to shareholders).","pages":[54]},{"text":"To achieve capital gain in the value of the company's shares (a rise in the value of the shares).","pages":[54]}]}]},
  "Managers and employees": {"d":[{"text":"Managers are the people who oversee the daily operations of a business.","pages":[53]},{"text":"Employees are the staff of an organization. They have a stake (an interest and involvement) in the organization they work for.","pages":[63]}],"kind":"Definitions"},
  "Customers": {"d":[{"text":"Customers are the clients of a business. As a key external stakeholder group, customers seek to have value for money, such as competitive prices and good quality products.","pages":[63]}]},
  "Suppliers and lenders": {"d":[{"text":"Suppliers are an external stakeholder group that provide a business with stocks of raw materials, component parts and finished goods needed for production. They can also provide commercial services, such as maintenance and technical support.","pages":[64]},{"text":"Financiers are the financial institutions and individual investors who provide sources of finance for an organization. They are interested in the organization’s ability to generate profits and to repay debts.","pages":[63]}],"kind":"Definitions"},
  "Government and local communities": {"d":[{"text":"Government refers to the ruling authority within a state or country. As an external stakeholder group, the government is interested in businesses complying with the law with regards to the conduct of business activities.","pages":[63]},{"text":"The local community refers to the general public and local businesses that have a direct interest in the activities of an organization, namely to create jobs and to conduct business activities in a socially responsible way.","pages":[63]}],"kind":"Definitions","lists":[{"heading":"Government interests","items":[{"text":"Unfair business practices are avoided.","pages":[57]},{"text":"The correct amount of corporate tax is paid from the net profits of the business.","pages":[57]},{"text":"Health and safety standards are met.","pages":[57]},{"text":"There is compliance with employment legislation.","pages":[57]},{"text":"Consumer protection laws are upheld.","pages":[57]}]}]},
  "Pressure groups": {"d":[{"text":"Pressure groups consist of individuals with a common concern (such as environmental protection) who seek to place demands on organizations to act in a particular way or to influence a change in their behaviour.","pages":[63]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Actions taken by pressure groups","items":[{"text":"Boycotting is the refusal to buy products from a certain business as a sign of protest, by creating adverse publicity for the business.","pages":[56]},{"text":"Lobbying means using the power of pressure groups to influence key issues with employers, legal representatives and the government, such as petitioning the government to legislate against socially undesirable business activities.","pages":[56]},{"text":"Public Relations (PR) involves getting positive publicity about a specific opinion or cause. For example, charities often use famous celebrity ambassadors to support their mission.","pages":[56]},{"text":"Direct action means a pressure group takes action to achieve its cause, such as staging mass protests or taking legal action against firms that may have behaved illegally.","pages":[56]}]}]},
  "Competitors": {"d":[{"text":"Competitors are the rival businesses of an organization.","pages":[56]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Objectives","items":[{"text":"Businesses need to be aware of and respond to the activities of their rivals.","pages":[56],"point":"To remain competitive"},{"text":"Businesses will want to compare key performance indicators against their main rivals (such as sales turnover, profit and market share figures) in order to remain relevant.","pages":[56,57],"point":"To benchmark performance"},{"text":"Businesses might benefit from some competition as rivalry can create incentives to keep up to date with changes in the internal and external environments (see Chapter 46), such as new production processes or product launches.","pages":[57],"point":"To be creative and innovative"}]}]},
  "Stakeholder conflict": {"d":[{"text":"Stakeholder conflict refers to differences in the varying needs and priorities of the various stakeholder groups of a business.","pages":[64]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Dealing with conflicting stakeholder needs","items":[{"text":"A partnership (see Chapter 2) might strive for profit maximization, whereas a non-profit organization is likely to have different priorities.","pages":[58],"point":"The type of business entity"},{"text":"If a firm targets growth and expansion as part of its business strategy, then the proportion of profits allocated to its owners will be less important, at least in the short-term.","pages":[58],"point":"The goals and objectives of the business"},{"text":"Customers will have more power if the business is selling a product in a mass market where there are plenty of substitutes widely available.","pages":[59],"point":"The source and degree of power (influence) for each stakeholder group"}]},{"heading":"Benefits of meeting stakeholder needs","items":[{"text":"For example, addressing the needs of both employees and managers can lead to a highly cohesive, motivated and loyal workforce with low rates of staff absenteeism and labour turnover.","pages":[60]},{"text":"This can lead to improved customer relations, a better corporate image, higher market share and greater profits in the long-term. As a result, shareholders will also be pleased.","pages":[60]},{"text":"Furthermore, greater output might also lead to more employment opportunities in the local community.","pages":[60]}]}]},
  "Stakeholder mapping": {"d":[{"text":"Stakeholder mapping is a model that assesses the relative interest of stakeholders and their relative influence (or power) on an organization.","pages":[64]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Stakeholder mapping lets managers assess how to deal with changing and conflicting stakeholder objectives. Whilst it is extremely difficult to please all stakeholders at the same time, managers can prioritize their actions by using this model.","pages":[62]}]}]},
  "Reasons to grow": {"d":[{"text":"Growth is a strategic objective for many businesses.","pages":[84]}],"kind":"Textbook passage","lists":[{"heading":"Benefits of being a large business","items":[{"text":"These are cost-saving benefits due to operating on a larger scale, which reduces the firm’s average cost of production.","pages":[73],"point":"Economies of scale"},{"text":"Larger firms are able to offer customers greater price discounts through their ability to enjoy economies of scale.","pages":[73],"point":"Lower prices"},{"text":"Familiarity with the brand allows large businesses to sell to a wider market. Many firms are large and established enough to benefit from global brand recognition.","pages":[73],"point":"Brand recognition"},{"text":"Larger firms tend to be more trusted due to their brand image and brand reputation.","pages":[73],"point":"Brand reputation"},{"text":"Larger firms have the resources to provide a wider range of services for their customers, such as longer opening hours and interest-free credit instalments.","pages":[73],"point":"Value- added services"},{"text":"Larger firms can provide more choice for their customer. For example, Amazon offers a larger range of books, toys and music compared to a small local book shop, toy store, or music retailer.","pages":[73],"point":"Greater choice"},{"text":"The above benefits mean that more customers are likely to remain loyal to the business, its products and its brands due to the perceived trust and overall value for money.","pages":[73],"point":"Customer loyalty"}]}]},
  "Reasons to stay small": {"d":[{"text":"The optimal (most appropriate) size for a business depends on its internal structure, its finances and its aims and objectives.","pages":[75]}],"kind":"Textbook passage","lists":[{"heading":"Reasons for staying small as a business","items":[{"text":"Large scale operations can mean that a firm encounters diseconomies of scale due to problems of control, coordination and communication. Owners of small firms might not want to expand as they could face higher unit costs.","pages":[74],"point":"Cost control"},{"text":"External growth through methods such as mergers and acquisitions (M&As) and takeovers may result in the dilution of ownership and control for the original owners.","pages":[74],"point":"Loss of control"},{"text":"As the costs of running a large global business are huge (such as the costs of research and development, marketing, recruitment and training), the financial risks are also high. By contrast, owners of small businesses can better manage and control their finances.","pages":[74],"point":"Financial risks"},{"text":"Financial support in the form of grants and subsidies can be offered to small businesses to help them start up and to develop. Funds for training may also be available for small businesses that provide employment opportunities in the local community.","pages":[74],"point":"Government aid"},{"text":"Small businesses may enjoy being the only firm in a particular location, such as a local restaurant, a franchised petroleum retailer, or a small convenience store located in a remote town. Large businesses may be reluctant to locate in remote areas (see Chapter 38), so this provides an opportunity for smaller firms to establish themselves in the area.","pages":[74],"point":"Local monopoly power"},{"text":"Smaller firms are more likely to have the time to devote to individual customers. For example, staff at a small local convenience store can get to know their customers better as employees are not pressurised by high sales targets.","pages":[74],"point":"Personalised services"},{"text":"Small businesses tend to be more flexible and adaptive to change. If a sole trader runs a beauty salon that is unsuccessful, then s/he might change the business to something completely different, such as a children’s toy shop.","pages":[74],"point":"Flexibility"},{"text":"Some businesses, such as a local hair salon or private tuition firm, are unlikely to attract the attention of large firms due to the very limited size of the market. Large corporations may not find it financially worthwhile to compete with these small local firms, thereby enabling them to thrive.","pages":[74],"point":"Small market size"}]}]},
  "Internal growth (organic growth)": {"d":[{"text":"Internal growth (also known as organic growth) occurs when a business grows using its own capabilities and resources to increase the scale of its operations and sales revenue.","pages":[87]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"It is often easier to grow internally than to rely on external sources. Internal growth also enables the organization to maintain control, whereas external growth can lead to a loss of control and ownership of the business.","pages":[70],"point":"Better control and coordination"},{"text":"The main source of internal growth is retained profits. There might also be a need to secure interest-bearing loan capital to fund the growth, but there is less risk involved with internal growth as the amount of capital tends to be lower.","pages":[70],"point":"Relatively inexpensive"},{"text":"A major problem for mergers and acquisitions is that when two or more firms with very different cultures work together to create a new company. By contrast, internal growth means there are no problems related with culture clashes (see Chapter 11) or conflicting management styles.","pages":[70],"point":"Maintains the corporate culture"},{"text":"Due to the above reasons, internal growth is the easiest and least risky method of growth and evolution for most businesses. Furthermore, internal growth builds on the strengths of the organization, such as its brand value and customer loyalty.","pages":[70],"point":"Less risky"}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"Higher average costs of production can arise from internal growth. Hierarchical structures (see Chapter 8) tend to be a feature of internal growth, causing communication problems and slower decision-making as a business grows.","pages":[70],"point":"Diseconomies of scale"},{"text":"Although a sole trader can control and coordinate the business quite easily, if it grows into a multinational company then the organizational structure has to be changed. Restructuring takes time, effort and money, such as training needs.","pages":[70],"point":"A need to restructure"},{"text":"If a firm grows by changing its legal status, say from being a partnership to a publicly held company, the owners (the partners) will have to share decision-making power with the new owners (shareholders). With more owners, decision-making is prolonged and conflicts between the different stakeholders are more likely.","pages":[70],"point":"Dilution of control and ownership"},{"text":"Internal growth is slower than external growth. Despite the risks, shareholders may prefer more rapid methods of growth (such as mergers, acquisitions, takeovers or franchising, in order to increase their return on investment.","pages":[70],"point":"Slower growth"}]}]},
  "External growth": {"d":[{"text":"External growth (or inorganic growth) occurs when a business grows and evolves by collaborating with, buying up or merging with other organizations.","pages":[86]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"External growth tends to be a faster way to grow and diversify as external resources and finances are used.","pages":[71],"point":"Quicker than organic growth"},{"text":"Businesses can benefit from a greater pool of skills, knowledge and the expertise of external parties.","pages":[71],"point":"Synergies"},{"text":"External growth is a relatively quick, albeit expensive, method of reducing the degree of competition and raising the firm’s market share.","pages":[71],"point":"Reduced competition"},{"text":"Rapid external growth can help businesses to gain access to larger markets and hence economies of scale from operating on a larger scale.","pages":[71],"point":"Economies of scale"},{"text":"External growth enables businesses to benefit from diversification. Hence, firms face fewer risks overall from failures in any particular aspect of its business operations.","pages":[71],"point":"Spreading of risks"}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"M&As, JVs, strategic alliances and franchising tend to be more expensive than internal growth methods.","pages":[71],"point":"More expensive than internal growth"},{"text":"Inadequate knowledge of new markets and the greater uncertainties of external growth create greater risks.","pages":[71],"point":"Greater risks"},{"text":"External growth, such as acquisitions and takeovers, can be blocked by governments if the move is deemed to be anti-competitive.","pages":[71],"point":"Regulatory barriers"},{"text":"Increased complexities of internal growth can equally cause inefficiencies and hence a rise in average costs.","pages":[71],"point":"Potential diseconomies of scale"},{"text":"It is often difficult to combine different cultures and management styles, especially if external growth leads to a change in the nature and culture of a business.","pages":[71],"point":"Organizational culture clash"}]}]},
  "Internal economies of scale": {"d":[{"text":"Internal economies of scale occur within a particular organization (rather than the industry as a whole) as it grows in size.","pages":[87]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Types of internal economies of scale","items":[{"text":"Large firms can use sophisticated capital and machinery to mass produce their products. The high fixed costs of their equipment and machinery are spread over the huge scale of output, thereby reducing the average cost of production.","pages":[66],"point":"Technical economies"},{"text":"Large firms can borrow large sums of money at lower rates of interest compared to smaller competitors because the larger organizations are seen as less risky to financial lenders.","pages":[66],"point":"Financial economies"},{"text":"Managerial economies By contrast, large firms divide managerial roles by employing specialist managers. The higher productivity means that average costs can fall further.","pages":[67]},{"text":"This is similar to managerial economies of scale but results from division of labour of the workforce, rather than the management.","pages":[67],"point":"Specialization economies"},{"text":"Large firms can benefit from a lower average cost by selling in bulk, thus benefiting from time savings and transactions costs.","pages":[67],"point":"Marketing economies"},{"text":"Large firms can also lower their average costs by buying resources in bulk.","pages":[67],"point":"Purchasing economies"},{"text":"These savings can be enjoyed by conglomerates (firms with a diversified portfolio of products in different markets). Unfavourable trading conditions for certain products or industries can be offset by more favourable conditions in other sectors of the conglomerate.","pages":[67],"point":"Risk-bearing economies"}]}]},
  "Purchasing economies": {"d":[{"text":"Purchasing economies of scale occur when larger organizations can gain huge cost savings per unit by purchasing vast quantities of stocks (raw materials, components, semi-finished goods and/or finished goods).","pages":[87]}]},
  "Technical and managerial economies": {"d":[{"text":"Technical economies of scale are cost savings by greater use of large-scale mechanical processes and specialist machinery, such as mass production techniques which help to cut average costs of production.","pages":[87]},{"text":"Managerial economies - A sole trader (see Chapter 2) often has to fulfil the functions of marketer, accountant and production manager. As people cannot be equally good at everything, specialisation leads to higher productivity. By contrast, large firms divide managerial roles by employing specialist managers.","pages":[67]}],"kind":"Textbook passages"},
  "Financial economies": {"d":[{"text":"Financial economies of scale are cost savings made by large firms as banks and other lenders charge lower interest (for overdrafts, loans and mortgages) because larger businesses represent lower risk.","pages":[86]}]},
  "Marketing economies": {"d":[{"text":"Marketing economies - Large firms can benefit from a lower average cost by selling in bulk, thus benefiting from time savings and transactions costs.","pages":[67]},{"text":"Global firms such as McDonald’s and Nike can spread the high costs of their advertising by using the same marketing campaign across the world (language translation is a minor part of the costs of a global marketing budget).","pages":[67]}],"kind":"Textbook passage"},
  "Specialization economies": {"d":[{"text":"Specialization economies of scale occur when larger firms can afford to hire and train specialist workers, thus helping to boost their level of output, productivity and efficiency.","pages":[87]}]},
  "Risk-bearing economies": {"d":[{"text":"Risk bearing economies of scale occur when large firms can bear greater risks than smaller ones due to having a greater product portfolio.","pages":[87]}]},
  "Optimal level of output": {"d":[{"text":"The optimal level of output is the most efficient scale of operation for a business. This occurs at the level of output where the average cost of production is minimized.","pages":[87]}]},
  "External economies of scale": {"d":[{"text":"External economies of scale occur when an organization's average cost falls as the industry grows. Hence, all firms in the industry benefit.","pages":[86]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Examples of external economies","items":[{"text":"Technological progress increases the productivity level within the industry. For example, the Internet has created huge cost savings for businesses engaged in e-commerce.","pages":[67]},{"text":"Improved transportation networks help to ensure prompt deliveries. Furthermore, employees who are late to work due to poor transportation links cost the business money.","pages":[67]},{"text":"An abundance of skilled labour might exist in the local area, perhaps through government aided training programmes or reputable education and training facilities in a certain location. This provides local businesses with a suitable pool of educated and trained labour, thereby helping to cut recruitment and training costs (see Chapter 7) without compromising productivity levels.","pages":[68]},{"text":"Regional specialisation means that a particular location or country has a highly regarded and trustworthy reputation for producing a certain good or service. This allows the industry to benefit from having access to specialist labour, sub-contractors and suppliers, thus helping to reduce the average cost of production for the industry.","pages":[68]}]}]},
  "Internal diseconomies of scale": {"d":[{"text":"Internal diseconomies of scale occur due to internal problems of mismanagement, causing average costs of production to increase as a firm grows.","pages":[87]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Causes of internal diseconomies of scale","items":[{"text":"As a firm becomes larger, managers may lack control and coordination as the span of control (see Chapter 8) is likely to increase and cause communication problems. Ultimately, these difficulties slow down decision-making.","pages":[68]},{"text":"There is likely to be poorer working relationships in an oversized business. With a larger workforce, senior managers are more likely to become detached from those lower down in the organizational hierarchy, thereby making them feel distanced or out of touch.","pages":[68]},{"text":"Outsized organizations are likely to suffer from the disadvantages of specialization and division of labour. Workers become bored with performing repetitive tasks.","pages":[68]},{"text":"The amount of bureaucracy (excessive administration, paperwork and company policies) is also likely to increase as a business grows. This makes decision-making more time consuming and adds to production costs but is unlikely to contribute to a proportional rise in the output of goods and services.","pages":[68]},{"text":"Complacency with being a large and dominant firm with market power or even being the market leader can also cause many problems. Complacency (a lack of awareness of genuine risks or deficiencies) is most likely to reduce productivity thereby raising unit costs of production.","pages":[68]}]}]},
  "External diseconomies of scale": {"d":[{"text":"External diseconomies of scale occur due to factors beyond its control which cause average costs of production to increase as an industry grows.","pages":[86]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Causes of external diseconomies of scale","items":[{"text":"Too many businesses locating in a certain area causes land to become even more scarce thereby causing higher rents. This adds to the fixed costs of all businesses in the area without any corresponding increase in output.","pages":[69]},{"text":"Since workers have greater choice from a large number of employers in the local area, businesses might have to offer higher pay and financial rewards to retain workers or attract new staff. This will increase costs without necessarily increasing output, thereby raising average costs of production for all firms in the industry.","pages":[69]},{"text":"Traffic congestion results from too many businesses being located in an area. This increases transportation costs for businesses, thereby contributing to an increase in unit costs of production.","pages":[69]}]}]},
  "Merger": {"d":[{"text":"A merger is a form of external growth whereby two (or more) firms agree to form a new organization, thereby losing their original identities.","pages":[87]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Benefits of mergers and acquisitions","items":[{"text":"The integrated company is likely to benefit from having greater market power, higher sales revenues and a larger customer base.","pages":[78],"point":"Greater market share"},{"text":"Operating on a larger scale helps to lower unit costs of production, thus improving the firm's competitiveness and/or profit margins.","pages":[78],"point":"Economies of scale"},{"text":"The integrating firms have access to each other's resources, such as distribution channels, technologies, human resources and management expertise. Hence, they are able to make use of their combined resources to increase productivity, sales revenues and profits.","pages":[78],"point":"Synergy"},{"text":"M&As are a quick method of growth to protect the survival of a business. As a defensive strategy, it allows the new firm to be in a stronger position to compete with its rivals.","pages":[78],"point":"Survival"},{"text":"Some M&As allow firms to diversify their product mix. This allows them to spread and reduce risks as well as benefit from a larger customer base.","pages":[78],"point":"Diversification"},{"text":"This can be achieved if the companies involved in the M&A currently operate in different markets.","pages":[78],"point":"Gain entry into new markets"}]},{"heading":"Drawbacks of mergers and acquisitions","items":[{"text":"Job losses are likely to occur due to cost savings in M&As. For example, the new company will not need two finance or marketing directors.","pages":[78],"point":"Redundancies"},{"text":"There are likely to be disputes and differences between the companies involved, so conflict is inevitable even if they eventually agree to the M&A.","pages":[78],"point":"Conflict"},{"text":"People will need to adapt to the desired corporate culture of the newly formed organization. The M&A, as a new entity, might also entail changes to the company's core values and mission statement.","pages":[78],"point":"Culture clash"},{"text":"The original owners or Board of Directors will lose some degree of control as the new Board will need to be restructured, including the creation of a new management team.","pages":[78],"point":"Loss of control"},{"text":"The larger firm might suffer from increased bureaucracy and longer channels of communication, leading to less effective decision-making.","pages":[78],"point":"Diseconomies of scale"},{"text":"Governments may be concerned with and hence prevent, M&As if they create a monopoly with too much market power.","pages":[78],"point":"Regulatory problems"}]}]},
  "Acquisition": {"d":[{"text":"An acquisition is a method of external growth that involves one company buying a controlling interest (majority stake) in another company, with the agreement and approval of the target company's Board of Directors.","pages":[86]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Benefits of mergers and acquisitions","items":[{"text":"The integrated company is likely to benefit from having greater market power, higher sales revenues and a larger customer base.","pages":[78],"point":"Greater market share"},{"text":"Operating on a larger scale helps to lower unit costs of production, thus improving the firm's competitiveness and/or profit margins.","pages":[78],"point":"Economies of scale"},{"text":"The integrating firms have access to each other's resources, such as distribution channels, technologies, human resources and management expertise. Hence, they are able to make use of their combined resources to increase productivity, sales revenues and profits.","pages":[78],"point":"Synergy"},{"text":"M&As are a quick method of growth to protect the survival of a business. As a defensive strategy, it allows the new firm to be in a stronger position to compete with its rivals.","pages":[78],"point":"Survival"},{"text":"Some M&As allow firms to diversify their product mix. This allows them to spread and reduce risks as well as benefit from a larger customer base.","pages":[78],"point":"Diversification"},{"text":"This can be achieved if the companies involved in the M&A currently operate in different markets.","pages":[78],"point":"Gain entry into new markets"}]},{"heading":"Drawbacks of mergers and acquisitions","items":[{"text":"Job losses are likely to occur due to cost savings in M&As. For example, the new company will not need two finance or marketing directors.","pages":[78],"point":"Redundancies"},{"text":"There are likely to be disputes and differences between the companies involved, so conflict is inevitable even if they eventually agree to the M&A.","pages":[78],"point":"Conflict"},{"text":"People will need to adapt to the desired corporate culture of the newly formed organization. The M&A, as a new entity, might also entail changes to the company's core values and mission statement.","pages":[78],"point":"Culture clash"},{"text":"The original owners or Board of Directors will lose some degree of control as the new Board will need to be restructured, including the creation of a new management team.","pages":[78],"point":"Loss of control"},{"text":"The larger firm might suffer from increased bureaucracy and longer channels of communication, leading to less effective decision-making.","pages":[78],"point":"Diseconomies of scale"},{"text":"Governments may be concerned with and hence prevent, M&As if they create a monopoly with too much market power.","pages":[78],"point":"Regulatory problems"}]}]},
  "Takeover": {"d":[{"text":"A takeover (also referred to as hostile takeover) occurs when a company buys a controlling interest in another firm without the prior agreement or approval of the target company's Board of Directors.","pages":[87]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Reasons why businesses become takeover targets","items":[{"text":"They have growth potential but lack sufficient funds for internal growth.","pages":[80]},{"text":"They are seen as a small rival that has growth potential.","pages":[80]},{"text":"They have a widely recognised corporate name or brand but currently face a liquidity or financial crisis.","pages":[80]},{"text":"They are vulnerable (an easy target for a takeover) due to a drop in profits and subsequent fall in their share price.","pages":[80]}]}]},
  "Horizontal integration": {"d":[{"text":"Horizontal integration is an external growth strategy that occurs when a business amalgamates with a firm operating in the same stage of production.","pages":[87]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Horizontal M&As do not represent growth in the industry but a larger market share and hence greater market power (dominance), for the integrated business.","pages":[77]}]}]},
  "Vertical integration": {"d":[{"text":"Vertical integration takes place between businesses that are at different stages of production.","pages":[87]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Types of vertical integration","items":[{"text":"Forward vertical integration is a growth strategy that occurs with the amalgamation of a firm operating at a later stage in the production process, such as a book publisher acquiring book retailers.","pages":[87]},{"text":"Backward vertical integration occurs when a business amalgamates with a firm operating in an earlier stage of production, such as a car manufacturer taking over a supplier of tyres or other components.","pages":[86]}]},{"heading":"Benefit of backward vertical integration","items":[{"text":"This external growth strategy helps the manufacturer to secure lower costs of raw materials.","pages":[77]}]}]},
  "Lateral integration": {"d":[{"text":"Lateral integration refers to external growth of firms that have similar operations but do not directly compete with each other, such as PepsiCo acquiring Quakers Oats Company.","pages":[87]}]},
  "Conglomerate integration": {"d":[{"text":"Conglomerate M&As are the amalgamation of businesses that operate in completely distinct or diversified markets.","pages":[78]}]},
  "Synergy": {"d":[{"text":"Synergy is a benefit of growth, which occurs when the whole is greater than the sum of the individual parts when two or more business operations are combined. Synergy creates greater output and improved efficiency.","pages":[87]}]},
  "Demerger": {"d":[{"text":"A demerger occurs when a company sells off a part of its business, thereby separating into two or more businesses. It usually happens due to conflicts, inefficiencies and incompatibilities following an earlier merger of two or more companies.","pages":[86]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Reasons for downsizing","items":[{"text":"Offloading unprofitable sections of the business.","pages":[85]},{"text":"Avoiding rising average costs and inefficiencies by being too large (caused by internal and external diseconomies of scale).","pages":[85]},{"text":"Raising cash to sustain operations in other parts of the business that are more profitable.","pages":[85]},{"text":"Having a clearer corporate focus by concentrating efforts on a smaller range of products or business operations.","pages":[85]}]},{"heading":"Drawbacks of downsizing","items":[{"text":"For example, there is likely to be a reduction in staffing, so redundancies have to be planned and managed sensitively and ethically.","pages":[85]},{"text":"For larger organizations, the costs of staff redundancies can be quite considerable and cause short-term liquidity problems for the business (see Chapter 20).","pages":[85]}]}]},
  "Joint venture": {"d":[{"text":"A joint venture is a growth strategy that combines the contributions and responsibilities of two or more different organizations in a shared project by creating a separate legal enterprise.","pages":[87]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"The pooling of experiences, skills and resources of the collaborating firms in the JV should create synergies.","pages":[80],"point":"Synergy"},{"text":"Financial costs, risks and losses are shared in a JV thereby helping to reduce the financial burden on any single organization. JVs also allow firms to diversify their products, thereby helping to spread the risks associated with growth and evolution.","pages":[81],"point":"Spreading of costs and risks"},{"text":"Joint ventures are used by companies to enter foreign countries by forming an agreement with local firms. In some cases, national laws make JVs the only option for businesses wishing to enter foreign markets.","pages":[81],"point":"Entry to foreign markets"},{"text":"Mergers and acquisitions can be highly expensive as they entail lengthy legal procedures and administrative costs. By contrast, JVs are cheaper to establish and are easier to pull out of should this be necessary.","pages":[81],"point":"Relatively cheap"},{"text":"Competition is reduced by forming a JV. Companies cooperating in a JV are unlikely to directly compete with each other, yet their pooled resources make them a stronger force against their rivals.","pages":[81],"point":"Competitive advantages"},{"text":"Firms that expand via international JVs can take advantage of each other's local knowledge and reputation. This might not be the case with mergers or acquisitions that are exposed to potential problems of overseas expansion (such as differences in business etiquette, cultural values, languages and local traditions).","pages":[81],"point":"Exploitation of local knowledge"},{"text":"Joint ventures tend to be friendly and well received by key stakeholder groups. The parties pool their funds and resources, sharing responsibility for their mutual benefit.","pages":[81],"point":"High success rate"}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"Partners in a JV have to rely heavily on the resources and goodwill of their counterparts.","pages":[81]},{"text":"There is also likely to be a dilution of the brands, yet firms spend huge amounts of money trying to develop their own brands (see Chapter 27).","pages":[81]},{"text":"Finally, whenever companies work together on a project, there is always the possibility of organizational culture clashes that can lead to operational problems and conflicts for the joint venture.","pages":[81]}]}]},
  "Strategic alliance": {"d":[{"text":"Strategic alliances are formed when two or more organizations join together to benefit from external growth, without having to set up a new separate legal entity.","pages":[87]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"The main purpose of a strategic alliance is to gain synergies from the different strengths of each member of the consortium by pooling their resources (to gain from each other's areas of expertise as well as financial support).","pages":[82]},{"text":"Strategic alliances also enable members to gain from economies of scale by operating on a larger scale.","pages":[82]},{"text":"Furthermore, customers are likely to benefit from the value-added services under a strategic alliance, such as the convenience of access to wider channels of distribution (see Chapter 30).","pages":[82]}]},{"heading":"Drawbacks shared with joint ventures","items":[{"text":"Joint ventures and strategic alliances (see below) have similar drawbacks. Partners in a JV have to rely heavily on the resources and goodwill of their counterparts.","pages":[81]},{"text":"There is also likely to be a dilution of the brands, yet firms spend huge amounts of money trying to develop their own brands (see Chapter 27).","pages":[81]},{"text":"Finally, whenever companies work together on a project, there is always the possibility of organizational culture clashes that can lead to operational problems and conflicts for the joint venture.","pages":[81]}]}]},
  "Franchising": {"d":[{"text":"Franchising refers to an agreement between a franchisor selling its rights to other businesses (franchisees) to allow them to sell products under its corporate name in return for a fee and regular royalty payments.","pages":[87]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Benefits for the franchisor","items":[{"text":"The company can experience rapid growth without having to risk large amounts of money as the franchisee pays for the rights. Hence, it can be cheaper and faster than internal growth.","pages":[83]},{"text":"It allows the company to have a national or international presence without the higher costs of internal growth or M&As (as the franchisee helps to finance the expansion).","pages":[83]},{"text":"The franchisor benefits from rapid growth without having to worry about running costs such as recruitment, training and development, staff salaries and the purchase of stocks (inventories).","pages":[83]},{"text":"Franchisors receive royalty payments from the franchisee, usually set as a percentage of the sales revenues. They also usually charge franchisees a 'membership' (registration or joining) fee.","pages":[83]},{"text":"Franchisees have more incentives to do better than salaried managers, thereby increasing the chances of success for the franchisor.","pages":[83]}]},{"heading":"Drawbacks for the franchisor","items":[{"text":"There is a huge risk in allowing other parties (franchisees) to use the franchisor's name. Unsuccessful franchisees can damage the reputation of the entire franchise business.","pages":[84]},{"text":"It can be difficult to control the daily operations of all franchisees and to get them to meet the quality standards set by the franchisor.","pages":[84]},{"text":"Although franchising is faster than internal growth methods, it is not as quick as other external methods such as M&As.","pages":[84]}]},{"heading":"Benefits for the franchisee","items":[{"text":"There are relatively lower start-up costs because the business idea has already been developed by the franchisor (such as market research, brand development and product development strategies).","pages":[83]},{"text":"There is relatively low risk because the franchisor has a tried and tested formula, so the chances of business success are statistically high.","pages":[83]},{"text":"It is in the best interest of the franchisor to ensure that the franchise succeeds, so it will provide value-added services to support the franchisee, such as training and advice on financial management.","pages":[83]},{"text":"The franchisee is likely to benefit from large scale advertising used by the franchisor, i.e. franchisees receive 'free' advertising and promotion, which also helps to reduce their costs.","pages":[83]},{"text":"Franchisees are still, technically, their own bosses, so have a large degree of autonomy in running their own business, within the parameters of the agreement.","pages":[83]}]},{"heading":"Drawbacks for the franchisee","items":[{"text":"Franchisees cannot simply use their own initiative to try out new ideas as they are overseen by the franchisor and the formal agreement. This could hinder the entrepreneurial talents of the franchisee.","pages":[84]},{"text":"Buying a franchise can be very expensive, yet there is no guarantee that this investment will ever be recouped.","pages":[84]},{"text":"Franchisees have to pay a significant percentage of their sales revenues to the franchisor.","pages":[84]}]}]},
  "Ansoff matrix": {"d":[{"text":"The Ansoff matrix (1957) is a decision-making tool used to devise various product and market growth strategies, depending on whether businesses want to market new or existing products in either new or existing markets.","pages":[615]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"The Ansoff matrix is a decision-making tool that provides a simple way for managers and entrepreneurs to compartmentalise various growth strategies based on a business providing existing or new products in new or existing markets.","pages":[614]},{"text":"The tool prompts discussions about the potential risks and rewards of each potential growth strategy.","pages":[614]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, the tool does not quantify these risks and rewards (unlike decision trees, for example) nor does it actually suggest a particular strategy that the business should pursue (unlike forcefield analysis, for example).","pages":[614]},{"text":"Instead, any managerial preference that stems from using the Ansoff matrix needs further research and data.","pages":[614]},{"text":"In reality, firms are not limited to a single growth strategy. Large multinational companies competing in highly contestable markets need to use multiple business strategies to grow, evolve and remain competitive.","pages":[614]}]}]},
  "Market penetration": {"d":[{"text":"Market penetration is the growth strategy that involves a business choosing to focus on selling existing products in existing markets, i.e., to increase their market share of current products.","pages":[611]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"An advantage of market penetration is that the business focuses on markets and products that it is familiar with. Hence, market research expenditure (see Chapter 26) can be minimised.","pages":[611]},{"text":"It is also the safest of the four growth strategies.","pages":[611]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"A limitation of market penetration is that competitors, especially stronger rivals, are likely to retaliate to any business trying to take away their customers and market share. This can lead to aggressive reactions, such as price wars (see Chapter 28), thereby harming profits in the short term at least.","pages":[611]},{"text":"Also, once existing markets become saturated, alternative growth strategies are required if the business is to continue its growth and evolution.","pages":[611]}]}]},
  "Market development": {"d":[{"text":"Market development is the growth strategy that involves selling existing products in new markets, i.e., an established product is marketed to a new set of customers.","pages":[612]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"A key advantage of this growth strategy is that the business is familiar with the product that is being marketed. For example, Nike and Adidas successfully market their clothing products for leisure rather than specifically for sports.","pages":[612]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, the success of a product in one market does not necessarily guarantee its success in other markets. Market development is a medium risk growth strategy as it involves the business competing in new markets.","pages":[612]}]}]},
  "Product development": {"d":[{"text":"Product development is a growth strategy in the Ansoff matrix that involves introducing new products to existing customers by developing or replacing current products.","pages":[615]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Product extension and brand development","items":[{"text":"Product development tends to rely heavily on product extension strategies to prolong the demand for goods and services that have reached the saturation or decline stage of their product life cycle (see Chapter 27).","pages":[612]},{"text":"Product development is also reliant on brand development to appeal to the existing market. For example, well-established companies such as Sony or Nike can use their brand names to launch new products to the market as there are fewer risks of introducing a new product under a well-known and trusted brand.","pages":[612]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"Hence, this strategy carries a medium level of risk for the business.","pages":[612]}]}]},
  "Diversification": {"d":[{"text":"Diversification is a growth strategy in the Ansoff matrix, which involves an organization launching new products in new markets.","pages":[615]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"In addition to capturing market share in established markets, a key driving force for diversification is to spread risks by having a well-balanced product portfolio (see Chapter 27).","pages":[612]},{"text":"Diversification is also suitable for businesses that have reached saturation and are seeking new opportunities for growth.","pages":[612]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"Nevertheless, diversification remains the riskiest of the four growth options in Ansoff's matrix as the business is not operating in familiar territory when launching new products in markets it has little, if any, experience of.","pages":[613]},{"text":"Furthermore, new distribution channels (see Chapter 30) need to be established and this can be time consuming and costly.","pages":[613]},{"text":"Such distractions can also mean an organization loses focus of its core business with serious consequences.","pages":[613]}]},{"heading":"Types of diversification","items":[{"text":"Related diversification occurs when a business caters for new customers within the broader confines of the same industry.","pages":[613]},{"text":"By contrast, unrelated diversification refers to growth by selling completely new products in untapped markets.","pages":[613]}]}]},
  "Marketing": {"d":[{"text":"Marketing is the management process of predicting, identifying and meeting the needs and wants of customers, usually in profitable ways.","pages":[352]}]},
  "Market orientation": {"d":[{"text":"Market orientation is a marketing approach adopted by businesses that are outward looking by focusing on making products that they can sell, rather than selling products they can make.","pages":[352]},{"text":"It focuses on the customer in order to identify, design, develop and supply products that meet their needs and wants. Such information can be gathered from market research (see Chapter 26).","pages":[342]},{"text":"Market research and data analyses are therefore central to a market orientation approach.","pages":[342]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Businesses can respond quickly to changes in the market as they have access to relevant data and information about customers. Market orientated businesses are also more able to anticipate changing market trends and hence prepare for such changes.","pages":[342],"point":"Greater flexibility"},{"text":"Market orientated businesses can be more confident that their products will sell and be more successful. Decisions based on scientific methods, logic and reasoning tend to be of lower risk.","pages":[343],"point":"Lower risk"}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, the main disadvantage of market orientation is that market research (needed to find out what customers actually want) can be very expensive.","pages":[343]},{"text":"In addition, given the dynamic nature of the business environment and the uncertainty of the future, there is no guarantee that this approach will work.","pages":[343]}]}]},
  "Product orientation": {"d":[{"text":"Product orientation is a marketing approach used by businesses that are inward looking as they focus on selling products that they can make, rather than making products that they can sell.","pages":[352]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"The main advantages of product orientation are that quality can be assured and the business has more control over its operations.","pages":[343]},{"text":"Also, by being innovative, product orientation can give organizations a competitive advantage or a unique selling point (see Chapter 24). For example, Apple gained a USP for its iconic iPhone's 'slide to unlock' technology when the device was launched and revolutionised the way people communicate worldwide.","pages":[343]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, as the needs of the market are ignored (because product orientated organizations assume they know what the market wants), there is a high failure rate of businesses that use this marketing approach. Hence, this strategy tends to be of high risk, especially due to frequent and continual changes in fashion and tastes.","pages":[343]},{"text":"The money spent on research and development of products without taking the customer into consideration often proves costly.","pages":[343]}]}]},
  "Market size": {"d":[{"text":"Market size refers to the magnitude of an industry, usually measured in terms of the value of sales revenue from all the businesses in a particular market, per time period.","pages":[352]}]},
  "Market share": {"d":[{"text":"Market share measures an organization's portion of the total value of sales revenue in a specific industry.","pages":[352]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages of a high market share","items":[{"text":"There is a positive relationship between market share and profits, although the business with the largest market share is not necessarily the most profitable.","pages":[344]},{"text":"High market share has other benefits such as the status enjoyed from being a dominant market player and the ability to gain a range of economies of scale (see Chapter 5).","pages":[344]},{"text":"In general, businesses with high market share (market leaders) have better price-setting ability and are less threatened by competition.","pages":[344]}]},{"heading":"Limitations of measuring market share","items":[{"text":"Furthermore, calculating market share is not necessarily a straightforward task because it is not always easy to define the industry for a particular good or service.","pages":[347]},{"text":"Furthermore, sales data are often out-of-date, so any market share information will represent a historical situation and does not necessarily signify the current position.","pages":[347]}]}]},
  "Market growth": {"d":[{"text":"Market growth refers to the rate at which the size of a market is increasing, typically expressed as the percentage increase in the market size of an industry per year.","pages":[352]}]},
  "Market leadership and the importance of share": {"d":[{"text":"Market leadership refers to the position of a business having the largest market share in a given market for a particular good or service.","pages":[346]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages of market leadership","items":[{"text":"Premium prices (see Chapter 28) can be charged due to the firm's market leadership status, which increases profit margins.","pages":[347]},{"text":"Lower production costs exist due to economies of scale as the market leader operates at a larger scale than its rivals in the industry. This means the business has the market power to lower prices, whilst maintaining profit margins, in order to attract even more customers.","pages":[347]},{"text":"The market leader enjoys longer product life cycles (see Chapter 27) due to the high degree of customer loyalty that exists for the brand or product.","pages":[347]},{"text":"The market leader benefits from favourable distribution terms as more retailers and intermediaries choose to sell or redistribute the products of well-known and trusted brands (see Chapter 30).","pages":[347]},{"text":"There is more publicity and brand exposure due to having market leadership status. Market leaders develop a reputation for being the preferred brand of customers and more talked about, leading to further word of mouth promotion (see Chapter 29).","pages":[347]},{"text":"It can also be easier to attract and recruit highly qualified employees as they feel proud to be associated with the status held by market leaders. For example, Google is consistently ranked as one of the best companies to work for by its employees.","pages":[347]}]},{"heading":"Pressures on a market leader","items":[{"text":"Nevertheless, being a market leader does add constant pressure for managers and employees to maintain a high standard and to continue to repeat success for the owners (shareholders) of the business.","pages":[347]},{"text":"Market challengers will also add pressure as they strive to become the market leader themselves through methods such as aggressive price reductions to attract customers.","pages":[347]}]}]},
  "Market concentration ratio": {"d":[{"text":"The concentration ratio is an indicator of the degree of competition in an industry by calculating the sum of the market share held by the largest specified number of firms in the industry.","pages":[352]}]},
  "Marketing planning": {"d":[{"text":"Marketing planning is a systematic process of devising marketing objectives and appropriate marketing strategies to achieve these goals.","pages":[370]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"The main advantage of marketing planning is that it improves an organization's chances of success. Of course, the marketing plan does not guarantee success, but it can help managers to identify and deal with anticipated problems.","pages":[354]},{"text":"The various functional areas of a business will also have a clearer idea of the organization's objectives and the constraints in which they are to operate.","pages":[354]},{"text":"Thus, a key role of marketing planning is to allow marketing managers to have better control of their operations.","pages":[354]}]},{"heading":"Limitations","items":[{"text":"For example, many small businesses do not have the time, resources or expertise to plan their marketing in such a systematic way. They react to (rather than anticipate and plan for) changes in the marketplace.","pages":[354]},{"text":"Even for large organizations, managers need to devote appropriate resources (time, people and finance) to marketing planning.","pages":[354]},{"text":"Marketing plans can be inflexible and become outdated quite quickly as they do not allow for sudden changes in market conditions. This is especially the case in high-tech and fast-paced industries.","pages":[354]}]}]},
  "Marketing audit": {"d":[{"text":"A marketing audit is a review of the current position of an organization's marketing mix, in terms of its strengths and weaknesses and consideration of opportunities and threats.","pages":[370]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"The marketing audit enables the business to set marketing goals and targets, such as improved competitiveness or increased market share.","pages":[354]}]}]},
  "Market segmentation": {"d":[{"text":"Market segmentation is the process of categorizing customers into distinct groups with similar characteristics (such as age or gender) and similar wants or needs.","pages":[370]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Market segmentation enables a business to better understand the needs and wants of its various customer groups. A clearer marketing strategy therefore reduces the chances of wasting resources on marketing products in the wrong places and to the wrong people.","pages":[356],"point":"Better understanding of customers"},{"text":"Being able to cater for a wider range of customers in a market can help a business to sell more products and hence earn more profit.","pages":[356],"point":"Higher sales revenues"},{"text":"Effective market segmentation can help businesses to identify new opportunities in local, regional and international markets.","pages":[356],"point":"Growth opportunities"},{"text":"Having a better understanding of different market segments allows a business to effectively differentiate its products and spread its risks by devising more appropriate marketing plans and marketing mixes for different subgroups of the market.","pages":[356],"point":"Support for product differentiation strategies"}]}]},
  "Targeting and the target market": {"d":[{"text":"A target market refers to a clearly identifiable group of customers that marketers choose to focus their efforts on, such as children, adults, men or women for a particular product.","pages":[370]},{"text":"Targeting refers to each distinctive market segment having its own specific marketing mix, depending on whether the business operates in niche or mass markets.","pages":[370]}]},
  "Market positioning": {"d":[{"text":"Once a business has determined the target customers for its goods and services, a positioning strategy is then needed. This requires the business to determine how best to differentiate each market segment and then to devise an appropriate marketing mix for each targeted segment.","pages":[360]}],"kind":"Textbook passage"},
  "Position map (perceptual map)": {"d":[{"text":"A product position map (or perception map) is a visual tool that reveals customer perceptions of a product or brand in relation to others in the market, often by comparing perceptions about price and quality.","pages":[370]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Position maps allow a business to identify any gaps in its product portfolio (see Chapter 27). For example, Mercedes-Benz introduced its A Class cars in 1997 after market research revealed there was a market for smaller cars that featured its luxury brand name.","pages":[361]},{"text":"Information in a perception map can also help businesses to refine their marketing strategies. For example, if customers perceive a particular brand to be of high price and high quality, then appropriate market segments can be targeted.","pages":[361]},{"text":"A major advantage of perception mapping is its simplicity in presenting potentially complex market research findings. The maps are quick and easy to interpret.","pages":[361]},{"text":"They can inform marketing managers about market opportunities and threats. For example, if undesired perceptions exist, the business will need to use appropriate marketing strategies to reposition its products.","pages":[361]}]}]},
  "Niche marketing": {"d":[{"text":"Niche marketing targets a specific and well-defined market segment, such as high-end speciality goods.","pages":[370]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"There is better focus as a specific market segment is targeted. By contrast, mass marketing has no real focus and markets products to all customers.","pages":[363]},{"text":"As there is less competition, businesses can charge higher prices for their unique or exclusive products. This helps the business to benefit from higher profit margins on its products.","pages":[363]},{"text":"Businesses become highly specialized in meeting the needs and wants of their niche target market. This can help to deliver first-rate customer service and encourages customer loyalty.","pages":[363]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"Niche markets are small which limits the number of potential customers in the market. By contrast, mass markets cater for a much wider customer base.","pages":[363]},{"text":"Due to the limited market size, businesses operating in niche markets have few, if any, opportunities to exploit economies of scale. Thus, average costs of product tend to be higher.","pages":[363]},{"text":"Successful and profitable niche markets attract new entrants into the industry. The threat of larger firms entering the market might endanger the survival of businesses operating in niche markets.","pages":[363]}]}]},
  "Mass marketing": {"d":[{"text":"Mass marketing refers to undifferentiated marketing. This marketing strategy ignores targeting individual market segments.","pages":[370]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Businesses can gain from huge potential economies of scale by supplying products in mass markets, thereby helping them to cut unit costs and/or raise profit margins.","pages":[364]},{"text":"There is no need to modify marketing strategies for different market segments as the whole market can be targeted with a single marketing campaign. This saves both time and resources.","pages":[364]},{"text":"Catering for larger (mass) markets means that the business can establish a larger customer base, thereby earning higher sales revenues and more profits.","pages":[364]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"Mass marketing is not suitable for all businesses due to the high barriers to entry required for mass production, such as high set-up costs.","pages":[364]},{"text":"Competition can become quite fierce as customers must be persuaded to buy the firm's products rather than to buy from a competitor. Thus, marketing budgets must be substantial to remain competitive.","pages":[364]},{"text":"As there is a lack of focus, mass marketing can be quite wasteful, as specific consumer groups are not being directly targeted.","pages":[364]}]}]},
  "Unique selling proposition (USP)": {"d":[{"text":"Unique selling point (or unique selling proposition) refers to any aspect of a good or service that makes it stand out (in a positive way) from those offered by competitors.","pages":[370]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"The USP explains why customers buy the product over rival ones, such as its distinctive features or appealing packaging.","pages":[365]},{"text":"Products with a USP are often promoted by word of mouth and social media (see Chapter 29), which also saves the business on its marketing budget.","pages":[365]},{"text":"A USP can be a major source of competitive advantage and therefore businesses want to emphasise their USP to attract customers, such as Pink Ladies which offers a female only taxi service in the UK (see Question 24.4).","pages":[365]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"Although having a USP can be advantageous, it can be extremely difficult to establish and maintain. In reality, once a business creates a successful USP in a market, others enter the market to compete.","pages":[365]}]}]},
  "Differentiation": {"d":[{"text":"Differentiation is the art of distinguishing a business or its products from rivals in the industry. It tries to create the perception among customers that the organization's product is different (unique or special) compared to substitute products in the market.","pages":[370]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Firms can only charge relatively low prices for mass produced and standardized products as customers have other substitute suppliers to choose from. By contrast, differentiation can add value to a firm's goods and services, thus allowing it to charge higher prices.","pages":[367],"point":"Price advantages"},{"text":"These can be a source of competitive advantage. High brand awareness creates more opportunities for products to be sold, perhaps due to customer loyalty or simply because customers feel more comfortable buying a familiar and trusted brand.","pages":[367],"point":"Brand recognition and brand loyalty"},{"text":"Retail space is limited, so vendors such as supermarkets and convenience stores only stock the best-selling brands. Therefore, successful product differentiation improves placement (distribution) of a firm's products.","pages":[367],"point":"Distribution advantages"}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"For example, special sales promotions can be highly expensive. Only large companies have the financial resources to differentiate their goods and services in order to target a larger number of customers in different market segments.","pages":[367],"point":"Differentiation can be very expensive"},{"text":"Cost savings cannot be fully exploited compared to mass production of a single, standardized product. Differentiation requires additional marketing costs, especially if the business caters for different customers in different markets.","pages":[367],"point":"Economies of scale"},{"text":"Excessive differentiation can drain a firm's resources and confuse customers, such as the pricing options used by mobile phone service providers or different levels of coverage provided by insurance companies.","pages":[367],"point":"Choice is not always desirable"}]}]},
  "Sales forecasting": {"d":[{"text":"Sales forecasting is a quantitative management technique used to predict a firm's level of sales over a given time period.","pages":[376]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Benefits","items":[{"text":"Sales forecasting can help a business to identify seasonal fluctuations in the demand for its products and hence the implications for the organization's liquidity position. If managers have a clear idea of expected costs and revenues, they are more likely to have better cash flow management in the coming months.","pages":[373],"point":"Improved working capital and cash flow (see Chapter 20)"},{"text":"Accurate sales forecasts help to ensure that the correct levels of stocks are available for use in production at different times of the year. Holding excessive or insufficient inventory can be a disaster.","pages":[373],"point":"Improved stock control"},{"text":"The ability to plan for the correct level of production means better use of an organization's resources. For example, many retailers temporarily hire part-time staff during peak holiday trading periods due to the high seasonal demand.","pages":[373],"point":"Improved productive efficiency (see Chapter 37)"},{"text":"Accurate and realistic sales forecasting can help a business to obtain external financing from investors and commercial lenders. This is especially important for new businesses as sales forecasts are a common requirement in business plans (see Chapter 48).","pages":[373],"point":"Helps to secure external sources of finance (see Chapter 15)"},{"text":"Accurate sales forecasting helps managers to anticipate changes, such as seasonal variations and therefore to adjust budgets accordingly. For example, changes in sales trends (the buying habits of customers) make it easier for production managers to know how much inventory to hold and for marketing managers to know how many sales staff to hire at different times of the year.","pages":[373],"point":"Improved budgeting (see Chapter 22)"}]},{"heading":"Limitations","items":[{"text":"Sales forecasting is a prediction based on historical data and trends. Sales forecasting does not reveal the whole picture (as the tool is based on assumptions), without any consideration of qualitative factors that affect actual sales.","pages":[374],"point":"Limited information"},{"text":"Critics argue that sales forecasting is part fact and part guesswork. There can be an element of bias or subjectivity in sales forecasting.","pages":[374],"point":"Inaccuracy of predictions"},{"text":"If the data and information used to predict sales forecasts are outdated, irrelevant or heavily biased, then the forecasts are unrealistic and of little or no value to management.","pages":[374],"point":"Garbage in, garbage out (GIGO)"},{"text":"The external business environment (see Chapter 46) causes change that may not be predictable, such as natural disasters, oil price hikes, fierce competition from abroad, unexpected fluctuations in the business cycle or adverse weather conditions.","pages":[374],"point":"External influences"}]}]},
  "Trend extrapolation": {"d":[{"text":"Extrapolation - This forecasting technique identifies a firm's sales trend by using historical data and extending this trend to predict future sales.","pages":[371]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Extrapolation works well if there is a clear correlation (relationship) between two data sets, such as sales revenue over a period of time or the correlation between marketing expenditure and sales growth.","pages":[371]}]},{"heading":"Limitations","items":[{"text":"Extrapolation is only useful (accurate) if predictions apply to the near future.","pages":[373]}]}]},
  "Time-series analysis": {"d":[{"text":"Time series analysis is a sales forecasting technique that attempts to predict sales levels by identifying the underlying trend from a sequence of actual sales figures.","pages":[376]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Limitations","items":[{"text":"Market research rather than time series analysis will be used during both the 'Research and Development' and 'Launch' stages of the product life cycle (see Chapter 27). More data and information become available during the growth and maturity stages of a product's life cycle.","pages":[373],"point":"The stage in a product's life cycle"}]}]},
  "Seasonality": {"d":[{"text":"Seasonal variations are periodic fluctuations in sales revenues during different times of the year.","pages":[376]}]},
  "Cyclical and random variations": {"d":[{"text":"Cyclical variations are recurrent fluctuations in sales revenues linked to the economic cycle of booms and slumps.","pages":[376]},{"text":"Random variations are unpredictable fluctuations in sales revenues caused by erratic and irregular factors that cannot be reasonably anticipated.","pages":[376]}]},
  "Simple linear regression": {"d":[{"text":"Simple linear regression is a statistical Business Management tool used to study the nature of the relationships between two variables.","pages":[724]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"As a quantitative tool, simple linear regression helps managers and entrepreneurs by tackling issues of complexity, volatility, ambiguity and uncertainty which are all associated with business decision making.","pages":[717]},{"text":"Importantly, simple linear regression is used as an estimation tool by managers, such as using the line of best fit in a scatter diagram to extrapolate (or predict) future outcomes.","pages":[722]},{"text":"Whilst linear regression can help to minimise the risks of decision making, you need to be aware of the potential limitations behind the data.","pages":[723]}]},{"heading":"Limitations","items":[{"text":"As with any quantitative or statistical tool, the benefits of using simple linear regression will depend on the validity of the data collected and used to calculate correlations and trends.","pages":[723]},{"text":"In particular, the line of best fit can be easily distorted by random variations and outliers in data sets (see Box 58.2).","pages":[723]},{"text":"Seasonal fluctuations in demand for certain goods and services can also create challenges in determining relationships and correlations (see Box 58.3).","pages":[723]},{"text":"Finally, it is important to remember that correlation does not always imply causation, irrespective of what the linear regression data might show. After all, a set of data conceals as much information as it reveals.","pages":[723]}]}]},
  "Market research and test marketing": {"d":[{"text":"Market research refers to marketing activities designed to discover the opinions, beliefs and preferences of potential and existing customers.","pages":[391]},{"text":"Test marketing is the trialling a new product with a sample of customers, perhaps in a limited geographical area, to determine the reactions of customers and to gather valuable feedback before a full launch.","pages":[410]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Benefits in forecasting","items":[{"text":"Identifying and forecasting the buying habits of consumers can be vital to a firm's prosperity and survival.","pages":[371],"point":"Market research"},{"text":"The data from test marketing can then be used to forecast sales prior to the product being launched nationally or internationally.","pages":[375,376]},{"text":"If the product is unsuccessful at this stage, the business can either make changes based on the feedback of customers or discontinue the product altogether.","pages":[399]}]},{"heading":"Limitations of test marketing","items":[{"text":"Although this strategy can be rather expensive and produce unrepresentative results, it reduces the risks of product launches.","pages":[376]},{"text":"However, test marketing can be expensive and competitors might find out about the product before its launch.","pages":[399]}]}]},
  "Market research": {"d":[{"text":"Market research refers to marketing activities designed to discover the opinions, beliefs and preferences of potential and existing customers.","pages":[391]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Purposes of market research","items":[{"text":"Giving businesses up-to-date information. This is particularly important in fast-paced industries that are always changing, such as the high-fashion and consumer electronics industries.","pages":[378]},{"text":"Enabling businesses to improve their marketing strategies by using a distinct marketing mix for each market segment or target market.","pages":[378]},{"text":"Assessing customer reactions to a new product by testing it on a small group of customers (focus groups). This can prevent huge losses had the product been unsuccessfully launched on a national or international scale.","pages":[378]},{"text":"Giving businesses a better understanding of the various activities and strategies used by their competitors.","pages":[378]},{"text":"Helping businesses to predict what is likely to happen in the future. Understanding the likely trends will enable businesses to react more accordingly in order to take advantage of future opportunities.","pages":[378]}]},{"heading":"Limitations of the findings","items":[{"text":"It is equally important to remember that research respondents are not always honest in their own responses to certain market research questions (such as their age, income level or their ability and willingness to pay certain prices for a product).","pages":[390]},{"text":"Cultural differences and people's suspicions of the intention of market researchers might also affect the responses given.","pages":[390]}]}]},
  "Primary research": {"d":[{"text":"Primary market research involves gathering new data for a specific purpose, using methods such as surveys, interviews, focus groups and observations.","pages":[392]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Primary market research is carried out for a specific purpose, so directly addresses the questions that need to be answered. By contrast, secondary data is not always in a format that is useful or usable for the business.","pages":[379],"point":"Relevance"},{"text":"Secondary market research data tends to be more dated and therefore often less reliable than primary research findings.","pages":[379],"point":"Up to date"},{"text":"As the market research is conducted first-hand, no one else (including competitors) has access to the information.","pages":[379],"point":"Confidential and unique"}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"It can be a very tedious and lengthy task to collect primary research data that are accurate and representative. This can delay decision-making and could lead to lost market opportunities in the short-term.","pages":[379],"point":"Time consuming"},{"text":"Collecting primary data is often costly, due to the time involved to gather sufficient and meaningful data or because data is collection is difficult.","pages":[379],"point":"Costly"},{"text":"Flaws in market research (such as poor questionnaire design or sampling errors) will lead to misleading, incomplete and biased results.","pages":[379],"point":"Validity"}]}]},
  "Secondary research": {"d":[{"text":"Secondary market research involves the collection of second-hand data and information that already exists, previously gathered by others, such as media articles and government publications.","pages":[392]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"If the data or information already exists, it is generally cheaper and faster to collect and analyse rather than primary market research that has to be designed and collected.","pages":[383]},{"text":"It can provide an insight to changes or trends in an industry, such as whether customers are spending more money on household goods, jewellery and tourism. This allows firms to develop strategies in response to these market changes.","pages":[383]},{"text":"There is a huge range of secondary sources that market researchers can use, especially with online sources. This makes secondary research data usually more accessible than primary research data.","pages":[383]},{"text":"Findings are often based on large sample sizes and thorough research techniques, so the results are statistically valid.","pages":[383]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"Secondary data is second-hand data, so the information might already be outdated or can become obsolete quite quickly, such as SWOT analyses (see Chapter 44) for the organization.","pages":[383]},{"text":"The data or information might be in an inappropriate format for the researcher as they have been collected for another purpose. Hence, the research needs to be further adapted or manipulated to suit the specific needs of the business.","pages":[383]},{"text":"Secondary research might only provide partial information as it was produced for a different purpose. This means the information does not address all the questions sought by the researcher.","pages":[383]},{"text":"Unlike primary research, the data and information from secondary market research are also available to competitors.","pages":[383]}]}]},
  "Random sampling": {"d":[{"text":"Random sampling involves giving everyone in the population an equal chance of being selected.","pages":[388]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"An advantage of using random sampling is that it is quite easy to get a sufficient sample.","pages":[388]},{"text":"Also, everyone has an equal chance of being selected so this might help to minimise potential bias or unrepresentative samples being selected (as can be the case with quota sampling).","pages":[388]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"The main drawback of random sampling is that it is indiscriminate, i.e. it might select people who are not part of the target group due to the randomness of selection.","pages":[388]},{"text":"Therefore, sample sizes need to be large enough to get representative and meaningful results. However, this can be both time-consuming and expensive.","pages":[388]}]}]},
  "Quota sampling": {"d":[{"text":"Quota sampling involves using a certain number of people (known as the quota) from different market segments for primary market research purposes.","pages":[392]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"An advantage of using quota sampling is that a relatively representative sample can be obtained quickly.","pages":[388]},{"text":"Also, the findings are more reliable than simply asking anyone on a random basis to participate in the market research.","pages":[388]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"The main disadvantage of quota sampling is that the number of people interviewed in each segment and how randomly they are chosen for interview, are not always representative of the population. For example, if a researcher had a quota of 100 female customers to interview, then the first 100 women might be asked to take part in the market research, without considering whether these respondents are representative of the population.","pages":[388]},{"text":"Sampling errors are therefore likely to occur since not everyone gets an equal chance to be part of the market research. Sampling errors are caused by mistakes made in the sample design, such as an unrepresentative sample being used or the sample size being too small to get statistically valid findings.","pages":[388]}]}]},
  "Convenience sampling": {"d":[{"text":"Convenience sampling uses research participants who are easy (convenient) to reach. It relies on the ease of reach because of the convenient availability of volunteers.","pages":[391]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"The main advantage of convenience sampling is the ease (availability and the quickness) of data collection. It is particularly useful when time and cost are key factors for market researchers or if they want to quickly determine whether further market research is necessary.","pages":[389]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, the main disadvantage is that market researchers inadvertently exclude a large proportion of the population by using convenience sampling, thus the findings are often highly skewed and unrepresentative of the population. For example, a study conducted during a weekday afternoon to determine the average amount that customers spend at a restaurant is unlikely to give the same results as if the study was conducted during the weekend or a public holiday.","pages":[389]}]}]},
  "Population, sampling frame and bias": {"d":[{"text":"The population, in marketing terms, refers to all potential customers of a particular market.","pages":[392]},{"text":"A sample is a selected group or proportion of the population used for primary market research purposes.","pages":[392]},{"text":"The respondents are often randomly chosen by a computer using information from a database.","pages":[388]},{"text":"Sampling errors (such as bias or an inadequate sample size) will hinder the validity of the findings.","pages":[389]}],"kind":"Textbook passages"},
  "Qualitative research": {"d":[{"text":"Qualitative market research involves getting non-numerical responses from research participants in order to understand their behaviour, attitudes and opinions.","pages":[392]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"It is better than quantitative research for exploring the driving forces (motivators) and restraining forces (demotivators) concerning the behaviour and attitudes of respondents.","pages":[386]},{"text":"Information gathered from qualitative research can be very valuable. Unlike the use of surveys, there is flexibility in the process so extra and useful information from in-depth interviews can be gathered.","pages":[386]},{"text":"Due to the low number of respondents involved in qualitative market research, it can be inexpensive yet provides detailed information to the researchers.","pages":[386]},{"text":"With one-to-one interviews, respondents are not under the pressure of conforming to the views and opinions of the majority. This should help to generate more honest and representative answers.","pages":[386]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"Due to the small sample size typically used in qualitative research, the findings might not be representative of the whole population. Further research might therefore be necessary.","pages":[386]},{"text":"It can be very time consuming to conduct and to interpret the findings. Quantitative responses are easier and quicker to collate than qualitative responses.","pages":[386]},{"text":"A high level of interviewing expertise is required to engage and encourage respondents. The costs of hiring skilled interviewers can therefore be high.","pages":[386]},{"text":"Interviewer bias might be introduced to serve the researcher's own purpose (to deliberately skew the results). Hence, the validity of the findings from qualitative market research can be questionable.","pages":[386]}]}]},
  "Quantitative research": {"d":[{"text":"Quantitative market research is about collecting and using factual and measurable information rather than people's perceptions and opinions.","pages":[392]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"However, as the findings can be collated and represented numerically, the results are usually easier to analyse.","pages":[387]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"For example, quantitative market research methods do not have the flexibility of qualitative methods because pre-set questions are used.","pages":[387]}]}]},
  "Surveys and questionnaires": {"d":[{"text":"A survey (sometimes referred to as a questionnaire) is a document that contains a series of questions used to collect data for a specific purpose. This is the most common method of primary market research.","pages":[379]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"A major advantage of using surveys is the ability to generate quantitative and qualitative answers specific to the needs of the researcher.","pages":[381]},{"text":"If designed properly, they can also be very simple to complete thereby making the process easier to gather market research data.","pages":[381]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, using surveys for market research can be very costly and time-consuming as it is necessary to use a large enough sample to get statistically representative findings.","pages":[381]},{"text":"There might also be reservations about the results due to bias or dishonest answers from some respondents.","pages":[381]}]},{"heading":"Advantages of personal surveys","items":[{"text":"The interviewer can address any questions that might arise from the survey (such as clarifying what certain questions mean).","pages":[379]},{"text":"It is also quicker for interviewers to complete the survey due to their familiarity with the questions and purpose of the survey.","pages":[379]}]},{"heading":"Telephone surveys · advantage","items":[{"text":"The benefit of this method is that a larger number of people in a wider geographical spread can be covered.","pages":[379]}]},{"heading":"Telephone surveys · disadvantages","items":[{"text":"The main drawbacks are the relatively higher costs and the low response rate (as a large number of people are not willing to take part in telephone surveys).","pages":[379]}]},{"heading":"Advantages of online surveys","items":[{"text":"They are much cheaper than other forms of primary market research such as paper-based surveys or telephone surveys.","pages":[379]},{"text":"Computer software can also help researchers to collate quantitative research data, thereby saving a lot of time and resources.","pages":[379]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages of postal surveys","items":[{"text":"A drawback of this method is that people might simply treat the surveys as junk mail, so ignore them.","pages":[379]},{"text":"To create an incentive for people to return postal surveys, businesses often provide free postage (the business pays for this) or offer prizes and gifts (but this clearly adds to the firm's costs).","pages":[379]},{"text":"Another limitation is the time delay between posting surveys to potential respondents and receiving the completed forms.","pages":[379]}]},{"heading":"Self-completed surveys · benefit","items":[{"text":"The information can help the business to identify problems, trends and suggestions for improvement.","pages":[379]}]},{"heading":"Postal surveys · convenience","items":[{"text":"Postal surveys are sent to people's home or office address for them to complete in their own time.","pages":[379]}]}]},
  "Interviews": {"d":[{"text":"Interviews are a type of primary research that involve discussions between an interviewer and interviewees to investigate their personal circumstances, preferences and opinions.","pages":[391]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Beliefs, attitudes and feelings can also be explored in detail.","pages":[381]},{"text":"The findings from all interviews are analysed to identify the views that respondents share. Identifying the differences can also be important as it can help a business to refine its marketing strategies.","pages":[381]},{"text":"In-depth interviews are often used when a business is planning to introduce change and new initiatives as they can be used to investigate the views of respondents to a new proposal.","pages":[381]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, interviews often provide a range of non-quantifiable information that might prove to be difficult to analyse or to make any extrapolations from.","pages":[381]},{"text":"In addition, interviews can be extremely time consuming to plan, conduct and analyse.","pages":[381]},{"text":"There is also huge scope for interviewer and interviewee bias, which can have an impact on the way in which respondents answer and how the results are therefore interpreted.","pages":[381]}]}]},
  "Focus groups": {"d":[{"text":"Focus groups involve forming small discussion groups to gain insight into the attitudes and behaviour of respondents. The group is typically made up of participants who share a similar customer profile.","pages":[391]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"They can provide important information to help a business to devise and refine its marketing strategies. For example, focus groups are often used when a business plans to launch a new product.","pages":[381]},{"text":"By using a focus group, detailed questions can be asked and participants are more likely to engage in discussions to generate insightful information.","pages":[381]},{"text":"Members of a focus group are often used for regular, ongoing market research. By using the same group, it saves the business from having to find new respondents.","pages":[381]},{"text":"Also, as the participants are targeted consumer groups, such as food critics (food tasters), they are particularly useful for conducting market research that requires specialist knowledge rather than using a random sample.","pages":[381]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"One drawback of using focus groups is that only extroverts tend to take part; those who shy from group discussions and debates are unlikely to participate and therefore their views are not acknowledged or represented.","pages":[381]},{"text":"In an open forum, there might also be some pressure for focus group members to conform to the majority view rather than to express their personal opinions.","pages":[381]},{"text":"Another potential limitation is that participants often have to be paid, either in cash or in lieu (such as a meal voucher) for their time and input. This expense raises the overall costs of using focus groups for market research.","pages":[381,382]}]}]},
  "Observation": {"d":[{"text":"Observations are a method of primary research that involves watching how people behave or respond in different situations.","pages":[391]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"A benefit of using observations is that they record people's actual behaviour rather than what people say they would do in a particular situation (as in the case of interviews and surveys).","pages":[382]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, observations do not necessarily reveal why a person behaves or responds in the way they do. The only way to establish the reasons behind a person's motivation is to ask them, either in a direct or indirect way.","pages":[382]}]}]},
  "Market analyses": {"d":[{"text":"A market analysis is a form of secondary market research that reveals the characteristics, trends and outlook for a particular product or industry, such as market size, market share and market growth rate.","pages":[391]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"The main advantage of using market analyses is that they enable businesses to access relevant up-to-date and detailed market data, trends and forecasts.","pages":[383]},{"text":"It can help to measure how well a business is doing compared with its competitors in the market.","pages":[383]},{"text":"New businesses often rely on market analyses to formulate their business plans (see Chapter 48).","pages":[383]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, it can be very expensive to use market analyses, as they usually have to be paid for. For example, most of the data and analyses on Statista are only available to subscribers who have paid for the service.","pages":[383]},{"text":"In addition, data and information from market analyses can become outdated quickly, unless the source provides regular and ongoing updates.","pages":[383]}]}]},
  "Academic journals": {"d":[{"text":"Academic journals are periodical publications from educational and research institutions that publish data and information relating to a particular academic discipline.","pages":[391]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"An advantage of using academic journals is that the articles contain the most up-to-date research in an academic discipline.","pages":[384]},{"text":"The source is likely to be the most reliable as it has been authored by academic scholars in a rigorous way. For example, the articles will have been proofread and checked by academic peers and editors.","pages":[384]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, disadvantages include the fact that information or data are not always relevant to the researcher and can become outdated quickly.","pages":[384]},{"text":"The findings are often contestable (see TOK question below).","pages":[384]},{"text":"Also, universities and academic establishments tend to charge users to access their academic journals.","pages":[384]}]}]},
  "Government publications": {"d":[{"text":"Government publications are a type of secondary market research, referring to official documents and publications released by government entities and agencies.","pages":[391]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"The main advantage of using government publications is that the data and statistics are usually comprehensive, reliable and up-to-date.","pages":[384]},{"text":"In addition, government publications cover a wide range of topics, thus providing researchers with a wealth of data, statistics and information.","pages":[384]},{"text":"Also, many government publications are available free of charge.","pages":[384]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, the main disadvantage of government publications is the challenge in identifying and locating the information required, due to the vast amount of information available.","pages":[384]},{"text":"There is often a fee for researchers to obtain specialist and detailed information from government publications.","pages":[384]}]}]},
  "Media articles": {"d":[{"text":"Media articles are a type of secondary market research referring to the documents (articles) in print or online media. They are written by skilled journalists and authors.","pages":[391]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"As media articles are released very frequently, a major advantage for researchers is that the data and information are up to date at the time of publication.","pages":[385]},{"text":"With advances in digital and mobile technologies, many media articles can be found online without people needing to take out a subscription to access such resources (a lot of this is funded by advertising revenues).","pages":[385]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, a disadvantage is the potential bias from the reporters, journalists and authors of the media articles.","pages":[385]},{"text":"Also, the articles can become out of date quite quickly and therefore irrelevant, rather quickly.","pages":[385]},{"text":"Finally, many media articles require users to have a paid subscription in order to access the information. This can therefore be a costly source of secondary market research data and information.","pages":[385]}]}]},
  "Online content": {"d":[{"text":"Online secondary market research refers to sources available on the Internet for research purposes. These include media articles, government publications, academic journals and market analyses available on the Internet.","pages":[392]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"The Internet can provide a range of invaluable information, if not a good starting point to find other sources of secondary market research data and information.","pages":[385]},{"text":"Unlike print media, online sources can benefit from the inclusion of audio-visual engagement, such as documentaries like Super-Size Me, An Inconvenient Truth, Freakonomics and Food, Inc.","pages":[385]},{"text":"A major advantage of using online sources is that they are readily available, 24 hours a day, every day. With advances in Internet technologies, researchers have access to websites and apps of countless businesses, large newspaper companies and news broadcasters from across the world.","pages":[385]},{"text":"In addition, the growing popularity of social media and social media marketing (see Chapter 29) allows news to be easily spread and shared via social networks (such as Facebook, Instagram, LinkedIn, Twitter and WhatsApp).","pages":[385]}]},{"heading":"Cost and availability","items":[{"text":"Online providers of secondary market research data and information often charge for their services, although careful and tactful use of the Internet can reap plenty of useful information without cost to the researcher, such as company websites, blogs and social media forums.","pages":[385]}]}]},
  "The seven Ps of the marketing mix": {"d":[{"text":"However, in addition to the traditional marketing mix for physical goods (price, product, promotion and place), the marketing of services includes three additional elements: people, processes and physical evidence. Together, these form the 7 Ps model in the marketing mix of services, first suggested by Bernard Booms and Mary Bitner in 1981.","pages":[453]}],"kind":"Textbook passage"},
  "Product": {"d":[{"text":"Product refers to any physical or non-physical item (good or service) that is purchased by commercial or private customers.","pages":[410]}]},
  "Product life cycle": {"d":[{"text":"The product life cycle refers to the typical process that products go through from their initial design and launch to their eventual decline and withdrawal at varying speeds.","pages":[410]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantage","items":[{"text":"The product life cycle (PLC) model allows managers to identify any necessary changes and to take appropriate action as part of an improved marketing strategy.","pages":[394]}]}]},
  "Research and development stage": {"d":[{"text":"The research and development stage involves the R&D department of an organization developing the ideas for a new product as well as the marketing department conducting thorough market research. Activities include idea generation, screening (deciding which ideas to develop further and which ones to drop), market research, market analyses, product development, testing of prototypes and test marketing.","pages":[395]}],"kind":"Textbook passage"},
  "Introduction stage": {"d":[{"text":"At this stage, the product is introduced to the market. It is likely that a significant amount of promotion is required (see Chapter 29) in order to raise awareness and to encourage sales.","pages":[395]}],"kind":"Textbook passage"},
  "Growth stage": {"d":[{"text":"During this stage of the product life cycle, the product enjoys sales growth and brand recognition. Promotional techniques may include the use of persuasive advertising so that customers understand why the product is better than its competitors.","pages":[395]}],"kind":"Textbook passage"},
  "Maturity stage": {"d":[{"text":"At this stage in the product life cycle, the rate of growth in sales slows down, although the firm has significant market share. The combination of high sales and low unit costs (due to economies of scale due to the firm's growth) leads to higher profits which can be used to support the development of other products in the firm's portfolio.","pages":[395]}],"kind":"Textbook passage"},
  "Decline stage": {"d":[{"text":"At the final stage in a product's life cycle, sale revenues and profits fall. Cost cutting become inevitable as there is no longer the need to use as many humans and financial resources to support the product.","pages":[396]}],"kind":"Textbook passage"},
  "Product life cycle, profit and cash flow": {"d":[{"text":"The phase of a product in its life cycle (see Figure 27.2) will have a direct impact on the firm's investment, profit and cash flow.","pages":[399]},{"table":{"caption":"Table 27.1 - The PLC and its relationship with investment, profit and cash flow","headers":["PLC stage","Investment level","Profit","Cash Flow"],"rows":[["R&D","Very high (research and development costs)","None","Highly negative"],["Launch","Very high (marketing)","Little, if any","Negative"],["Growth","High (persuasive promotions)","Yes, rising","Positive"],["Maturity","Low (mainly reminder promotions)","High, but little or no growth","Highly positive"],["Decline","Little, if any (extension strategies)","Yes, but falling","Positive, but falling"]]},"pages":[400]}],"kind":"Textbook passage"},
  "Product portfolio": {"d":[{"text":"Product portfolio refers to the collection of products owned by an organization at any one point in time.","pages":[410]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages of product portfolio management","items":[{"text":"Product portfolio management enables a firm to have better control over its sales revenues, costs, profits and operational risks.","pages":[396]},{"text":"Product portfolio management allows a business to decide which products need more (or less) financial support and investment. Products that do not have high market share may be withdrawn or remarketed, for example.","pages":[396]},{"text":"Product portfolio management also allows a business to develop appropriate growth strategies (see Chapter 5) by adding new products to an existing or new product range.","pages":[396]}]},{"heading":"Advantages of a balanced portfolio","items":[{"text":"A diverse product portfolio is important for an organization's cash flow because only selling to a single market limits the revenue streams of the business (see Chapter 16).","pages":[636]},{"text":"Having a balanced and diverse product portfolio also helps to spread risks because a decline in sales for one product may be offset by favourable sales of other products in the portfolio.","pages":[636]}]}]},
  "Boston Consulting Group (BCG) matrix": {"d":[{"text":"The BCG matrix is a situational and decision-making tool that helps managers plan for a balanced product portfolio (see Chapter 27).","pages":[635]},{"text":"The BCG matrix looks at two dimensions: market share and market growth in order to assess new and existing products in terms of their market potential (see Figure 47.1).","pages":[635]}],"kind":"Textbook passage","lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Product portfolio analysis allows a business to decide which products should receive more (or less) investment. Products that do not have high market share may be withdrawn or remarketed, for example.","pages":[636]},{"text":"The analysis also allows a business to develop appropriate growth strategies (see Chapter 45) by adding new products to an existing or new product range.","pages":[636]},{"text":"Product portfolio analysis and the BCG matrix can help managers to determine their product strategy. The tool suggests four different strategies to deal with stars, dogs, question marks and cash cows (see Figure 47.1).","pages":[637]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"A criticism of the BCG matrix is the assumption that higher profits come from higher sales or market share. This is not necessarily the case.","pages":[636]},{"text":"In addition, whilst the BCG matrix can provide a quick synopsis of a firm's product portfolio, it fails to explain the reasons for the position of the products in the grid. Hence, the BCG matrix (as with any other business management model) should be used in conjunction with other tools such as Ansoff's growth matrix (see Chapter 45).","pages":[637]}]}]},
  "Star": {"d":[{"text":"Stars are products that operate in high growth markets and have high market share. They generate high amounts of cash for a business.","pages":[396]}],"kind":"Textbook passage"},
  "Cash cow": {"d":[{"text":"Cash cows are products with high market share operating in a low-growth (mature) market. Such products are very well established, thereby generating large amounts of cash and of profits.","pages":[396]}],"kind":"Textbook passage","lists":[{"heading":"Risk","items":[{"text":"However, some cash cows run the risk of becoming dogs, so businesses may need to use extension strategies to prolong their high earning potential.","pages":[396]}]}]},
  "Question mark": {"d":[{"text":"Question marks are products that operate in a high market growth sector but have low market share. These products need additional marketing funding to increase market share.","pages":[396]}],"kind":"Textbook passage","lists":[{"heading":"Risk","items":[{"text":"Question marks are the main consumers of cash, so it is not always clear just how much a business should or can afford to invest in these products.","pages":[396]}]}]},
  "Dog": {"d":[{"text":"Dogs are products with low market share operating in a low growth market. These products do not generate much cash for the business because the market is stagnant or in decline, so businesses may need to use extension strategies or withdraw such products from the market.","pages":[396]}],"kind":"Textbook passage"},
  "Extension strategies": {"d":[{"text":"Extension strategies are attempts by marketers to lengthen the life cycle of a particular product, typically used during the maturity or early decline stages of the product's life cycle.","pages":[409]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Extension strategies","items":[{"text":"Such strategies tend to increase the demand for a product, especially if it is price elastic in demand due to the wide availability of substitutes (see Chapter 28). Businesses also use price cuts to get rid of excess stocks before they become obsolete.","pages":[398],"point":"Price reductions"},{"text":"Additional and new promotional campaigns, such as advertising and sales promotions, can help to attract new customers, remind previous customers that the product still exists and persuade or encourage existing customers to purchse more of the product.","pages":[398],"point":"Advertising"},{"text":"This extension strategy involves introducing special features or 'limited editions' to a current product. In theory, this entices more customers to purchase the product as there has been additional value added in the production process.","pages":[398],"point":"Redesigning"},{"text":"This involves changing the packaging of a product to help revive demand, perhaps by using more attractive colours or materials. The appearance of a product, including its packaging, can have a profound impact on demand (see Chapter 29).","pages":[398],"point":"Repackaging"},{"text":"Entering a new market for a current product can also extend its life cycle, such as trying to sell the existing product in new retail outlets, in different regions or even in overseas markets.","pages":[398],"point":"New markets"},{"text":"This refers to the use of an existing and successful brand name to launch a modified or new version of the existing product, thereby prolonging its life cycle.","pages":[398],"point":"Brand extension"},{"text":"These strategies can also help to prolong a product's life cycle. This refers to any marketing strategy that involves making a product stand out from others offered by rival businesses.","pages":[398],"point":"Product differentiation"},{"text":"This extension strategy is used if a product has suffered from negative publicity and falling sales. Changing the name of the brand product is a relatively straightforward way, in theory, to have a fresh start with the product remaining in its current format or slightly tweaked.","pages":[398],"point":"Change brand name"},{"text":"This extension strategy involves the business exploring new markets for the product. It might be possible to revive the demand for a product by creating a new use for it or by adding value so that it appeals to a different target market.","pages":[398],"point":"Reposition the product"}]}]},
  "Branding": {"d":[{"text":"Branding refers to the practice of using an exclusive name, symbol or design to identify a specific product or organization.","pages":[409]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Brand names create a legal identity for a product by giving it a unique and recognisable name and image to differentiate it from other products. As a legal instrument, branding gives lawful ownership to the business and protects it from imitations.","pages":[405],"point":"Branding is a legal instrument"},{"text":"Brands can give new products a better chance of survival. They can create a sense of value for money and encourage brand loyalty.","pages":[405],"point":"Branding is a risk reducer"},{"text":"Successful brands allow a business to charge a premium price (see Chapter 28) because customers are often willing to pay a substantially higher price for a 'good' brand. This also allows the business to earn higher profit margins.","pages":[405],"point":"Branding is an image enhancer"},{"text":"Branding can encourage brand development (including extension strategies) and brand loyalty. This enables the business to charge a proportionately higher price without losing customers, so it earns higher sales revenues despite charging higher prices (see Chapter 28).","pages":[405],"point":"Branding is a revenue earner"}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, developing a brand is very expensive and can take many years.","pages":[406]},{"text":"Most brands fail to ever become established in the marketplace, whilst many existing brands need regular investment or must evolve to survive.","pages":[406]},{"text":"Matt Haig, author of Brand Failures, found that highly successful brands can lose their value overnight because customer perception of products and brands can be fragile.","pages":[407]},{"text":"A successful branding strategy in one part of the world does not mean that the same formula will work in other places.","pages":[407]}]},{"heading":"How brands differ from products","items":[{"text":"Brands represent the intangible value that customers place on the actual physical product. Marketers argue that it is the brand that sells a product, not the other way round.","pages":[405],"point":"Intangibility"},{"text":"Brands are unique, whereas a product is quite easily copied.","pages":[405],"point":"Uniqueness"},{"text":"Successful brands are timeless, whereas products can become obsolete (as they reach the end of their life cycle). Sony's Walkman personal stereos and first-generation PlayStation games consoles were withdrawn in the late 1990s, but both brand names live on.","pages":[405],"point":"Timeless"}]}]},
  "Brand awareness": {"d":[{"text":"Brand awareness measures the extent to which people recognize a particular brand.","pages":[409]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Brand awareness plays a major part in the buying decision of consumers. For example, the Apple and Samsung brands have huge brand awareness and are often the first brands that people associate with smartphones and tablet computers.","pages":[402]},{"text":"In general, the higher the level of brand awareness, the higher sales revenues will be.","pages":[402]},{"text":"In addition, brand awareness gives the business a competitive edge over its rivals, resulting in greater market share.","pages":[402]},{"text":"It can also encourage repeat purchases if customers like and trust the brand.","pages":[402]}]}]},
  "Brand development": {"d":[{"text":"Brand development refers to the ongoing and long-term marketing process of improving and enlarging the brand name in order to boost sales revenue and market share.","pages":[409]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Both brand awareness and brand development help a business to stand out from other brands in the market.","pages":[402]},{"text":"Successful brand development helps to extend a product's life cycle. For example, Coca-Cola and Johnson & Johnson products were first launched in 1886, but remain highly successful brands today.","pages":[402]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"Whilst brand awareness can occur quite quickly, especially with an effective marketing strategy, it takes a lot longer to develop a brand and the desired image.","pages":[402]},{"text":"However, the costs of brand development can be extremely high.","pages":[402]},{"text":"There is a danger, however, that the generic use of a brand name leads to problems of enforcing copyright protection of the trademark.","pages":[402]}]}]},
  "Brand loyalty": {"d":[{"text":"Brand loyalty occurs when customers buy the same brand of a product repeatedly over time.","pages":[409]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"It helps businesses to maintain or improve their market share.","pages":[402]},{"text":"It enables businesses to charge premium prices for its products (see Chapter 28), which improves their profit margins.","pages":[402]},{"text":"It acts as a barrier to entry in highly competitive markets, such as the fashion (clothing) and consumer electronics industries. This is because brand loyalty reduces the likelihood of brand switching.","pages":[402]},{"text":"It plays a major role in the future success of a business, helping to prolong the product and brand's life cycles.","pages":[402]}]}]},
  "Brand value": {"d":[{"text":"Brand value refers to the premium that customers are willing to pay for a brand name over and above the value of the product itself.","pages":[409]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Market share is an indicator of the level of brand development and brand loyalty. There is a strong and positive relationship between a firm's market share and its level of brand value.","pages":[403],"point":"Higher market share"},{"text":"Having high brand value allows a business to charge higher prices for its products because customers feel that they are paying for the value added that the brand carries, such as the reputation or prestige of the brand. This gives customers reassurance that the product is genuine, reliable and of good quality.","pages":[403],"point":"Premium prices"},{"text":"Brand value makes it more difficult for new businesses to enter the market and compete, simply because customers are loyal to the existing brand. The dominance of brands such as Adidas, Coca-Cola, McDonald's and Rolex have created huge obstacles for rival brands.","pages":[403],"point":"Higher barriers to entry"}]}]},
  "Price": {"d":[{"text":"Price refers to the value of a good or service. It is the amount paid by a customer to purchase the product.","pages":[423]}]},
  "Cost-plus pricing": {"d":[{"text":"Cost-plus pricing (or mark-up pricing) involves adding a percentage or specific amount of profit to the cost per unit of output in order to determine the selling price.","pages":[423]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"The main advantage of cost-plus pricing is its simplicity and ease of calculation.","pages":[412]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, cost-plus pricing often relies too much on intuitive decision making, rather than on any actual market research about the needs of customers.","pages":[412]}]}]},
  "Penetration pricing": {"d":[{"text":"Penetration pricing involves setting low prices in order to gain entry into a new market. Once the product or brand has established market share, prices can be raised.","pages":[423]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"It allows the business to compete against existing firms in the industry and to gain market share.","pages":[412]},{"text":"Quite often, penetration pricing takes the form of a heavily advertised discounted price offer in order to attract a large number of customers in a short space of time.","pages":[412]},{"text":"The low price can also allow the firm to create brand awareness and brand recognition. As the firm or its product gets established and gains brand recognition, the price can be raised.","pages":[412]}]},{"heading":"Conditions","items":[{"text":"This strategy is suitable for mass market products that sell in large enough volumes to sustain low profit margins, such as fast-moving consumer goods sold in supermarkets.","pages":[413]},{"text":"It is also suitable for products that have a high price elasticity of demand (PED) whereby lowering price leads to proportionately higher sales volume.","pages":[413]}]}]},
  "Loss-leader pricing": {"d":[{"text":"Loss leader pricing involves setting the price of a good or service below its costs of production. The purpose is to entice customers to buy other products with high profit margins in addition to purchasing the loss leader product.","pages":[423]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"It is unlikely for customers to purchase only the loss leader products when visiting a supermarket, yet the existence of loss leaders can attract many more customers.","pages":[413]},{"text":"Loss leader products can also be used to encourage brand switching, which in the long term can make up for the losses incurred whilst the product was priced at a loss.","pages":[413]},{"text":"Loss leader pricing is also commonly used when a business wants to enter or penetrate a market. It is used to attract new customers to a good or service with the intention of establishing a customer base and securing future and recurring sales revenues.","pages":[413]}]},{"heading":"Cost of loss leaders","items":[{"text":"Games console makers such as Sony, Nintendo and Microsoft often sell their hardware at a loss to attract buyers. The aim is to recoup the loss by the subsequent and ongoing sales of complementary goods such as games and accessories, as well as revenues from subscription services and earning royalty payments from games manufacturers.","pages":[413]}]}]},
  "Predatory pricing": {"d":[{"text":"Predatory pricing involves temporarily setting prices so low that competitors, especially smaller businesses, cannot compete at a profitable level.","pages":[423]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"If the strategy is successful, the firm will benefit from being in a more dominant position in the market, so can therefore raise its prices to recoup any losses previously incurred.","pages":[413]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"Predatory pricing often stems from a price war, whereby businesses compete by a series of continuous and/or intensive price cuts. This might even mean selling products at below cost value.","pages":[413]},{"text":"Although price wars can bring some benefits to customers in the short term, predatory pricing itself is illegal in many parts of the world (such as in the USA and EU) because it is regarded as an anti-competitive trade practice.","pages":[413]}]}]},
  "Premium pricing": {"d":[{"text":"Premium pricing is when the price of a good or service is set significantly higher than similar competing products, usually because the product is of higher quality or is sufficiently unique to justify the premium price.","pages":[423]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Premium pricing has the advantage of generating higher profit margins for the business.","pages":[414]},{"text":"It can also create higher barriers to entry for competitors if it can establish a loyal customer base.","pages":[414]},{"text":"Businesses that use this pricing method, such as Nike and Gucci can also gain from increasing the brand's value for all their products.","pages":[414]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"A drawback of premium pricing is that it can limit the number of customers, due to the relatively high price.","pages":[414]},{"text":"Furthermore, premium brands may lose their status if they appeal to the mass market.","pages":[414]},{"text":"In addition, premium pricing requires strong brand loyalty, which can be expensive to establish and maintain. Ultimately, customers must be convinced that the product is worth the premium price.","pages":[414]}]}]},
  "Dynamic pricing": {"d":[{"text":"Dynamic pricing is the practice of varying the price of a good or service to reflect changing market demand, such as during different times of the day or year.","pages":[423]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Unlike the other pricing methods above, dynamic pricing is flexible and adaptive so that businesses are able to update their prices to capitalize on ever-changing market conditions, including offering price discounts when market demand is low and when the business has plenty of spare capacity.","pages":[415]},{"text":"In all cases, the use of surge pricing enables the business to generate higher levels of sales revenue.","pages":[415]},{"text":"Advantages of dynamic pricing include giving businesses greater control over their pricing method, with real-time data enabling firms to set the right (optimal) prices for different products. This will also enable the firm to maximize its profits.","pages":[415]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"A major disadvantage of dynamic pricing is that customers are often unhappy about not knowing just how high a price they have to pay for the good or service.","pages":[415]},{"text":"They often feel exploited (being overcharged). This can damage the reputation of the business and/or harm future sales.","pages":[415]},{"text":"For businesses, dynamic pricing can lead to price wars as firms try to undercut each other during off-peak periods to attract more customers. Price wars are not sustainable in the long-term and can even lead to bankruptcies.","pages":[415]}]}]},
  "Competitive pricing": {"d":[{"text":"Competitive pricing is the practice of a business setting the price of its goods or services at the same or similar level to that of its competitors.","pages":[423]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"An advantage of using competitive pricing is that it is simple and requires minimal effort.","pages":[416]},{"text":"By setting prices that are in line with rivals, the business can remain competitive.","pages":[416]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, the main disadvantage is that the business needs another way to attract customers, by using non-price methods to differentiate itself in a highly competitive market, such as advertising (to raise brand awareness) and providing superior customer service. This will clearly add to the firm's costs.","pages":[416]}]},{"heading":"Options for competitive pricing","items":[{"text":"This method could be suitable for a business that believes setting a higher price can help its brand image of being a superior product. However, in order to charge more, the business might need to provide extra features or services to justify the slightly higher price.","pages":[416],"point":"Pricing above the competition"},{"text":"When a business does this, the focus of competitive advantage is not price but brand differentiation, such as the special features of the product.","pages":[416],"point":"Pricing on the same level as the competition"},{"text":"this method could be suitable for businesses trying to survive in a highly competitive market. However, setting a lower price means lower profit margins, as well as potentially negatively affecting the brand's image.","pages":[416],"point":"Pricing below the competition"}]}]},
  "Contribution pricing": {"d":[{"text":"Contribution pricing is the practice of setting the selling price of a product higher than the direct costs of production per unit in order to ensure there is a positive contribution made towards payment of indirect costs.","pages":[423]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"The main advantage of contribution pricing is that it ensures the selling price is set high enough to cover both direct costs and contributes to the payment of indirect costs.","pages":[417]},{"text":"Essentially, contribution pricing ensures a business does not make a loss each time it makes a sale. This is particularly important when customers want to place a large order at a discounted price per unit.","pages":[417]},{"text":"For multi-product firms, each product in the product range that is sold will contribute a proportion to the payment of the firm's overall fixed costs, rather than any single product being accountable for all the indirect costs. Hence, contribution pricing allows a business to set different prices for different products based on their level of contribution.","pages":[417]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"A limitation of contribution pricing is that allocating indirect costs between different products can be subjective, which is deemed to be unfair or inequitable.","pages":[417]},{"text":"In addition, the business still needs to double-check the resulting contribution price to ensure that it remains competitive.","pages":[417]}]}]},
  "Price elasticity of demand (PED)": {"d":[{"text":"Price elasticity of demand (PED) measures the degree of responsiveness of demand for a product due to a change in the price of that product.","pages":[423]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Uses of price elasticity of demand","items":[{"text":"Helping firms to decide on their pricing policy, e.g., a business with price inelastic demand for its products is likely to raise prices. For example, airlines hike their prices during school holiday seasons, partly because there is increased demand and largely because firms know that the degree of sensitivity of demand to changes in price during peak periods is lower.","pages":[418]},{"text":"Determining which products are most affected by a downturn in the economy, e.g., luxury products are likely to have a high PED, so sales tend to suffer most during a recession.","pages":[418]},{"text":"Predicting the effects of exchange rate fluctuations, e.g., exporters will generally benefit from lower exchange rates as their prices become more competitive (so long as the PED for exports is price elastic).","pages":[418]},{"text":"Helping governments to determine the optimum level of taxes to impose on certain products, e.g., price inelastic products such as petrol and tobacco tend to be heavily taxed as demand is price inelastic. Whilst (price inelastic) demand for such products is not significantly affected by higher prices caused by the tax, the government manages to raise large sums of tax revenues to fund its expenditure.","pages":[418]}]}]},
  "Promotion": {"d":[{"text":"Promotion is a component of the marketing mix. It refers to the methods used to inform, persuade, and/or remind people about a firm's products and brands.","pages":[441]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Objectives of promotion","items":[{"text":"Informative promotion activities aim to alert the market about a firm's products, especially new or updated goods and services. Promotion might include information such as product features, functions and price.","pages":[425],"point":"Inform"},{"text":"Persuasive promotion activities aim to convince or encourage customers to make a purchase, to switch from rival products and to enhance brand loyalty.","pages":[425],"point":"Persuade"},{"text":"Reminder promotion refers to techniques used to retain customer awareness of, and interest in, an established product. Reminder promotional techniques are suitable for products that are in the maturity or saturation stages of their product life cycle (see Chapter 27).","pages":[426],"point":"Remind"},{"text":"Brand development promotional strategies help to build awareness, recognition, and loyalty to the business, its products, and its brands. It can be expensive for a business to earn a customer, so brand development strategies (see Chapter 27) are also important for retaining customers.","pages":[426],"point":"Develop"},{"text":"Promotional activities that aim to attract the attention of customers is a vital aspect of marketing. In particular, such promotional activities focus on attracting customers to the entire product range or family brand of products offered by the business.","pages":[426],"point":"Attract"}]},{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Successful promotion relies on the creativity of marketers. Given that competitors are likely to have similar resources and know-how, the originality and creativeness of a promotional strategy can give a business the competitive edge that it seeks.","pages":[439]},{"text":"Nevertheless, effective promotion can certainly improve the financial health and sustainability of a business.","pages":[439]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"Although promotional activities are important, especially during the early stages of a product's life cycle (see Chapter 27), they can also be extremely expensive. This is also partly because it often takes many attempts for a promotional message to be noticed or acted upon, if at all.","pages":[439]},{"text":"Critics argue that much of the expenditure on promotion is wasteful and could have been better spent on researching and developing better products.","pages":[439]}]}]},
  "Above-the-line promotion (ATL)": {"d":[{"text":"Above the line (ATL) promotion is any form of paid-for promotion through the mass media (such as television and radio) to reach a broad audience.","pages":[441]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"In summary, the main advantage of ATL promotion is that it reaches a potentially large number of customers.","pages":[429]},{"text":"Research has shown that customers tend to take more notice of ATL promotion as they are more interesting, engaging and appealing.","pages":[429]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, ATL promotion is highly expensive and might not even appeal to the audience. For example, many outdoor billboard adverts go unnoticed because they do not specifically target a market segment.","pages":[429]},{"text":"The success of ATL promotion is harder to measure than that of BTL promotion.","pages":[435]},{"text":"ATL methods use external agencies, and hence incur professional fees.","pages":[435]}]},{"heading":"Television advertising — advantages","items":[{"text":"Advertising on TV exploits the power of combining sound and moving images to bring about extremely powerful messages to audiences.","pages":[427]},{"text":"TV adverts can also be designed to meet specific needs, such as adverts aimed at children being aired during children's television programmes.","pages":[427]},{"text":"With advances in technology, such as high-definition digital television, it is possible to broadcast TV adverts to a global audience.","pages":[427]}]},{"heading":"Television advertising — disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, the major drawback is the huge costs of producing and broadcasting television commercials.","pages":[427]},{"text":"Television companies sell 'advertising slots' to businesses based on the level of demand (peak-time slots will obviously sell for a higher amount). Hence, marketers must try to get their marketing message across in an incredibly short time frame, usually within 30 seconds.","pages":[427]}]},{"heading":"Radio advertising — advantages","items":[{"text":"Radio advertising is able to reach an extremely large audience, yet it is significantly cheaper than TV advertising.","pages":[427]},{"text":"Unlike TV advertising, radio commercials do not rely on viewers having to be immobile in front of a TV screen. Instead, radio listeners are exposed to the promotions whilst they continue to do other things, such as driving, eating a meal or even whilst studying or working.","pages":[427,428]},{"text":"Advances in technology, such as wireless broadband, mean that digital radio commercials can be broadcast to almost anyone around the world.","pages":[428]}]},{"heading":"Radio advertising — disadvantages","items":[{"text":"The main drawback of radio advertising is that it can only communicate audio messages, i.e. there is no visual impact. Research has shown that people can retain verbal messages better when there is a visual stimulus.","pages":[428]},{"text":"Another related disadvantage is that audiences have lower attention levels compared to TV adverts which exploit the dynamics of moving images.","pages":[428]}]},{"heading":"Cinema advertising — advantages","items":[{"text":"A key advantage of using cinema advertising is that audiences can be directly targeted. Promotion can be tailored to specific market segments (such as the genre of film and age groups of the cinemagoers).","pages":[428]},{"text":"The size of cinema screens can exert more impact compared to other forms of promotion, especially with the increased popularity of 3D movies.","pages":[428]},{"text":"Furthermore, unlike television or radio commercials, marketers have a captured audience as it is much harder for viewers to ignore or switch off the adverts (especially as most cinemagoers perceive the commercials and trailers to be part of the overall cinema experience).","pages":[428]}]},{"heading":"Cinema advertising — disadvantages","items":[{"text":"The main drawback is its limited audience size compared to that of radio listeners and television viewers.","pages":[428]}]},{"heading":"Newspaper advertising — advantages","items":[{"text":"Newspaper advertising has the advantage of potentially reaching a wide audience yet is much cheaper than using TV advertising.","pages":[428]},{"text":"Unlike radio adverts, newspaper adverts can be referred to at a later date meaning that important information can be retained.","pages":[428]},{"text":"Although television and radio commercials today can be targeted at specific market segments (at least to some extent), newspaper adverts can target different markets better.","pages":[428]},{"text":"As newspaper firms have their own dedicated websites, they can have a much wider reach, which makes (digital) newspaper advertising more attractive.","pages":[428]}]},{"heading":"Newspaper advertising — disadvantages","items":[{"text":"The main constraint of newspaper advertising is the high costs, particularly for small businesses. Newspaper firms charge higher prices for adverts with colour, images or photos.","pages":[428]},{"text":"For a newspaper advert to stand out from the many others that appear, businesses may need to spend a lot more money than their rivals to ensure that readers are attracted to their advert.","pages":[428]},{"text":"Unlike other print media, such as magazines or outdoor posters, newspapers have a very short shelf life; people will not usually read yesterday's 'news', so many adverts are simply left unseen and unnoticed.","pages":[428]}]},{"heading":"Magazine advertising — advantages","items":[{"text":"Promotion in magazines has the advantage of being able to use high definition, photo-quality colour images to capture the attention of readers.","pages":[429]},{"text":"Targeting the right market segment is possible through the use of specialist magazines such as Car, Brides, PC Gamer and Vogue.","pages":[429]},{"text":"Adverts in magazines can be referred back to at a later date, which helps with brand recall and retention of marketing messages.","pages":[429]},{"text":"Magazines also have a longer shelf life than newspapers.","pages":[429]}]},{"heading":"Magazine advertising — disadvantages","items":[{"text":"One downside to using magazine promotion is that it is static. Hence, it is quite usual for a business to place several different adverts in the same magazine (think of all the various adverts used by fashion designers in clothing magazines, for example).","pages":[429]},{"text":"Also, readers are bombarded with advertisements (known as advertising clutter) so may unintentionally miss or deliberately ignore, the adverts.","pages":[429]},{"text":"A third disadvantage is that there can be a long lead-time between submitting an advert and its actual publication in a magazine (even in the case of digital magazine publications). This means there is a delayed period when sales are potentially lost unless additional methods of promotion are used.","pages":[429]}]},{"heading":"Outdoor advertising — advantages","items":[{"text":"With advances in technology, the ordinary outdoor advert has been transformed to billboards that automatically rotate (thereby increasing the number of promotions that can be shown on each physical billboard) and digital billboards that can combine moving images and sound. This has given outdoor advertising a dynamic dimension.","pages":[429]},{"text":"Another advantage is that there is a high rate of exposure, especially if businesses are able to use the same adverts in many different locations.","pages":[429]}]},{"heading":"Outdoor advertising — disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, a disadvantage of outdoor advertising is the difficulty in monitoring and measuring its effectiveness because targeting specific market segments is difficult (making it only suitable for promoting mass market products).","pages":[429]},{"text":"Traditional billboards are also prone to damage caused by bad weather, vandalism and graffiti.","pages":[429]},{"text":"central business districts such as New York City, Tokyo and London are swamped with billboard adverts.","pages":[429],"point":"Furthermore, there can be high levels of competition in terms of advertising clutter"}]}]},
  "Below-the-line promotion (BTL)": {"d":[{"text":"Below the line (BTL) promotion does not use paid-for mass media sources. Examples include the use of free samples (e.g. toiletries, food and drinks), discount vouchers (to entice customers to buy the product) and added-value promotions (e.g. special introductory deals).","pages":[441]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Below the line (BTL) promotion refers to the use of non-mass media promotional activities, allowing the business to have direct control. Unlike ATL promotion, this means that no commission has been paid to external media agencies.","pages":[430]},{"text":"BTL promotion specifically targets individual market segments as clearly identified potential customers.","pages":[430]},{"text":"These methods tend to be relatively cheap in relation to methods of ATL promotion.","pages":[430]},{"text":"The success of ATL promotion is harder to measure than that of BTL promotion.","pages":[435]}]}]},
  "Through-the-line promotion (TTL)": {"d":[{"text":"Through the line (TTL) promotion refers to promotional strategies that involve both above and below the line methods, enabling customers to engage with the product or brand in multiple ways.","pages":[441]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"This approach allows customers to engage with the product or brand in multiple ways, such as TV and radio adverts, direct sales marketing methods, social media platforms, and during a sponsored event. Hence, TTL promotion enables an integrated marketing approach where a consistent message across multiple platforms to inform, remind and persuade customers to purchase the product.","pages":[436]},{"text":"TTL benefits from the advantages of both ATL and BTL promotional methods by delivering marketing messages with a wide reach yet with focused customer engagements. This helps with brand visibility (getting the brand noticed and recognised) and brand recall (reminding customers of the benefits of the brand).","pages":[436]},{"text":"Technological advances, such as social media marketing (see below), have significantly broadened the opportunities available to businesses to extend their promotional strategies and intensified the use of TTL techniques in order to build brand awareness and develop brand loyalty.","pages":[436]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, TTL promotional strategies are more expensive to use than either ATL or BTL methods alone. For this reason, it is normally preferred by larger and more established businesses and financially secure organizations.","pages":[437]}]}]},
  "Social media marketing": {"d":[{"text":"Social media marketing (SMM) refers to the promotional practice of gaining online traffic through social media platforms such as Facebook, Twitter, LinkedIn, Instagram, YouTube and Google.","pages":[437]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Like WOM marketing, social media marketing is about spreading marketing messages for a particular product or brand from person to person but by using online technologies. SMM helps to build trust in a product or brand as the marketing message comes from a trusted, third-party source rather than from a commercial marketer.","pages":[438]},{"text":"In addition, the potential reach of using SMM is much greater and faster than WOM marketing due to Internet technologies.","pages":[438]},{"text":"SMM is also a highly cost-effective way to gain promotional exposure as the contents can be shared easily.","pages":[438]},{"text":"SMM enables marketing messages and information about specific topics or products to be shared with targeted audiences, usually on a regular basis, such as weekly film reviews or monthly newsletters.","pages":[437]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, the key drawback is that businesses have no control over what is written or shared about their brand, products or services. This means it has little if any control about negative publicity that may appear online.","pages":[438]},{"text":"Hackers also present a threat as they could deliberately spread false information about the business that can go viral, creating a public relations problem.","pages":[438]}]}]},
  "Sales promotion": {"d":[{"text":"Sales promotions are short-term incentives designed to stimulate interest in a product, such as the use of discount coupons, and prize draws.","pages":[441]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Sales promotion can help businesses to gain a short-term competitive edge, to get rid of excess or old stock and to build brand awareness.","pages":[431]},{"text":"Sales promotions can be beneficial to a business because they boost sales (at least in the short run).","pages":[432]},{"text":"They can also sway customers away from rival businesses and brands.","pages":[432]},{"text":"More importantly, sales promotion encourages action (the desire to make a purchase), rather than only informing or reminding customers about a product.","pages":[432]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, the main drawback of sales promotions is the cost. Free samples, discounts, prize draws, and so forth all add to the marketing costs of a business.","pages":[432]},{"text":"the greater the cost, the lower the profit margin tends to be.","pages":[432],"point":"Hence, sales promotions can reduce the profit margin on each product"},{"text":"In addition, sales promotions are only short-term tactics, so additional BTL strategies still need to be used.","pages":[432]}]}]},
  "Personal selling": {"d":[{"text":"Personal selling refers to promotional techniques that rely on sales representatives directly helping and persuading customers to buy, usually on a face-to-face basis.","pages":[441]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"A key benefit of using personal selling is that it can be tailored to the individual needs and preferences of the customer. The salesperson and the customer engage in dialogue to establish the customer's views and preferences, to answer any questions and to promote the organization's products to the customer.","pages":[431]},{"text":"Personal selling can develop customer engagement so also helps the business to build a positive, trusting and long-term relationship with customers.","pages":[431]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"A disadvantage of using personal selling is that sales agents can be expensive to hire. For example, sales representatives at health and beauty counters in large department stores are skilled in their product knowledge and craft, so are therefore remunerated accordingly.","pages":[431]}]}]},
  "Public relations and publicity": {"d":[{"text":"Public relations refers to marketing activities aimed at establishing and protecting the desired image of an organization. It is about getting positive media coverage, usually without directly paying for it.","pages":[441]},{"text":"Publicity is the process of promoting a business and its products by getting media coverage without directly paying for it.","pages":[432]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages of publicity","items":[{"text":"For example, famous celebrities are often snapped wearing designer labels, which gives those brands free publicity in the media.","pages":[432]}]},{"heading":"Role of public relations","items":[{"text":"PR experts are heavily relied upon when a business faces a crisis (see Chapter 41). They try to get the mass media to cover stories about the business in a positive, sympathetic and comforting way.","pages":[432]}]}]},
  "Sponsorship": {"d":[{"text":"Sponsorship is a promotional technique that involves funding, supporting or donating resources for an event or business venture in return for prominent publicity.","pages":[441]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Sponsorship involves a business providing financial funds and resources to support an event or another organization in return for publicity and prime advertising space and prominent publicity. For example, Arsenal Football Club received a €132m ($178m) sponsorship deal in 2006 with Emirates Airline. In return, Arsenal named its new stadium The Emirates Stadium.","pages":[433]},{"text":"Official sponsors such as Gillette, Yahoo! and Philips benefit most from having paid tens of billions of dollars to sponsor the FIFA World Cup because their products are promoted to audiences all across the world.","pages":[434]}]}]},
  "Direct marketing": {"d":[{"text":"Direct marketing refers to promotional activities that aim to sell a product straight to a customer rather than using an intermediary.","pages":[441]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"An advantage of direct marketing is that the business keeps a larger share of any financial returns as there are no intermediaries, such as advertising agents, to pay.","pages":[430]},{"text":"In addition, the business is free to promote its products in a way that it sees fit, rather than passing on control of this to an external agency.","pages":[430]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"A major drawback of direct marketing is the cost of producing and distributing promotional materials such as leaflets, brochures and catalogues.","pages":[430]},{"text":"Research also shows that most people ignore and dispose of 'junk' (unsolicited) mail, do not welcome unwarranted telephone calls from telesales personnel, and do not appreciate emails regarded as spam advertising.","pages":[430]},{"text":"Furthermore, direct marketing does not always reach the right audience, and hence represents a waste of resources. An example would be people who do not have young children but still receive mailed materials promoting schools, toys, children's books and clothes for toddlers.","pages":[430]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantage of a direct-mail database","items":[{"text":"In addition, the information in a database often goes out of date. For example, when people move home, they do not necessarily inform the business of this, resulting in a large amount of direct mail being sent to the wrong (old) address.","pages":[449]}]}]},
  "Point-of-sale promotion": {"d":[{"text":"Point of sale is the promotion of goods in retail stores at the place where customers can purchase the goods, such as promotional displays at supermarket counters.","pages":[441]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"whilst customers are queuing at the checkouts, they are exposed to the promotion of products such as batteries, confectionary items and magazines.","pages":[432],"point":"Supermarkets use this method extensively"},{"text":"Large and eye-catching in-store displays, stands and posters are used to encourage impulse buying (attracting customers to buy products that they had not intended to).","pages":[432]}]}]},
  "Trade shows": {"d":[{"text":"Trade shows are promotional events where firms exhibit and showcase their products for sale to potential customers.","pages":[441]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Trade shows (also referred to as trade fairs) enable exhibitors to conduct live demonstrations to showcase and promote their products.","pages":[432]},{"text":"The main advantage of using trade shows is that the exhibitions and events attract a large audience of targeted customers. This can certainly help the business with promoting its latest products to the right people.","pages":[432]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, there is likely to be competition as rivals also make an appearance at these events.","pages":[432]},{"text":"Exhibitors also need to consider the cost of joining trade shows. For example, the starting cost for an exhibitor at an IB Global Conference in 2019 (pre-pandemic) was $2,200 plus $895 per additional exhibitor attendee.","pages":[432]}]}]},
  "Word-of-mouth promotion": {"d":[{"text":"Word-of-mouth (WOM) promotion refers to the spreading of promotional information from one person to another through verbal communications.","pages":[441]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"A. Milligan and S. Smith (2002) argue in their book Uncommon Practice, that the fastest way to build a brand is through word-of-mouth promotion.","pages":[434]},{"text":"Therefore, WOM promotion is possibly the most effective form of promotion as information and recommendations about a product are passed onto friends and family without any direct costs to the business.","pages":[434]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, it can be potentially very damaging if the word spreads that a business or its products are sub-standard. This is ever-more important in today's digital world with online platforms for sharing customer feedback.","pages":[434]}]}]},
  "Guerrilla marketing": {"d":[{"text":"Guerrilla marketing, sometimes referred to as stealth marketing, uses untraditional, unconventional, and perhaps unruly but creative and original methods of promotion, on a relatively low budget.","pages":[434]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"As a BTL promotional strategy, guerrilla marketing is highly suitable for businesses that operate on a tight budget. An example is the use of simple promotional messages in public lavatories (washrooms), such as in urinals or on the mirrors.","pages":[434]},{"text":"Furthermore, the shrewd tactics used by guerrilla marketers can achieve big results. It aims to ambush or catch the attention of customers through unusual and/or shocking techniques.","pages":[434,435]},{"text":"The most effective guerrilla marketing strategies are simple, flexible, inexpensive and target specific market segments. Advances in technology such as the Internet have made guerrilla marketing more accessible to many businesses.","pages":[435]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"Unethical and controversial promotional strategies, including guerilla marketing, have prompted governments and pressure groups to introduce legislation and ethical codes of marketing practice. For example, consumer protection laws exist to prevent people from being deceived by misleading or dishonest promotions.","pages":[439]}]}]},
  "Packaging as promotion": {"d":[{"text":"Packaging is the art of presenting products in an advantageous way in order to improve sales.","pages":[441]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Apart from providing physical protection, packaging is also used to promote the brands and products of a business.","pages":[435]},{"text":"Almost all manufacturers and retailers use protective packaging, including carrier bags, for their products, displaying and therefore promoting the brand name or logo. Customers who reuse these bags are in effect helping to promote a business after the initial purchase has been made.","pages":[435]},{"text":"Packaging can also be used to encourage impulse buying (unplanned purchases). For example, confectionery and other food items sold in supermarkets often have eye-catching packaging to entice customers to purchase the product.","pages":[435]},{"text":"Nevertheless, there is little doubt that effective packaging can also have positive impacts on prices, customer perceptions and consumer demand.","pages":[435]},{"text":"It is also a form of product differentiation. For example, most jewellers offer a special wrapping service.","pages":[462]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"A main drawback of packaging is the cost. Customers are the ones who ultimately pay for the privilege of attractive packaging.","pages":[435]},{"text":"Environmentalists argue that marketers create excess packaging, at the cost of the environmental.","pages":[435]}]}]},
  "AIDA": {"d":[{"text":"The AIDA acronym can be used as a set of criteria for judging the success of a promotional campaign. It can be used to judge the extent to which the ATL, BTL or TTL promotional strategy draws the attention of the targeted audience, how it engages their interest, the intensity of the desire from potential customers, and ultimately the extent to which there is action taken to make a purchase.","pages":[437]}],"kind":"Textbook passage"},
  "Place and distribution channels": {"d":[{"text":"Place (or distribution) refers to the distribution of products, i.e., how products get from the producer to the consumer. It is the process of getting the right products to the right customers at the right time and place in the most cost-effective way Channels of distribution are the ways that a product gets from the manufacturer to the consumer, such as wholesalers, agents, retailers, e-commerce or vending machines.","pages":[452]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages of speciality distribution channels","items":[{"text":"As there are no intermediaries, the business does not have to share its profits with other entities.","pages":[448]},{"text":"Businesses can have direct control over their distribution, rather than relying on retailers, wholesalers and other intermediaries.","pages":[448]},{"text":"The growing popularity of e-commerce means that customers are more willing to use the Internet as a distribution channel, especially with improved online payment security and the convenience of apps on mobile devices.","pages":[448]},{"text":"Speciality distribution channels can reach potential customers who do not have easy access to retail outlets.","pages":[448]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages of a long distribution channel","items":[{"text":"A long channel of distribution tends to raise prices for consumers as each intermediary will add a profit margin to the price.","pages":[444]},{"text":"Furthermore, a longer channel of distribution lengthens the distribution process, meaning that such processes are not appropriate for perishable products, such as fresh milk, flowers, fruits and vegetables.","pages":[444]}]}]},
  "Direct distribution (zero intermediaries)": {"d":[{"text":"A zero-level distribution channel (also known as direct distribution) leaves out any intermediaries, i.e., the producer sells directly to the consumer.","pages":[452]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Direct selling, without the use of intermediaries, reduces the costs of distribution.","pages":[450]},{"text":"As there are no intermediaries, the business does not have to share its profits with other entities.","pages":[448]},{"text":"Businesses can have direct control over their distribution, rather than relying on retailers, wholesalers and other intermediaries.","pages":[448]},{"text":"New technologies have supported the shortening of the distribution chain and encouraged firms to sell directly to the consumer. Reducing costs in this way also enables firms to lower prices.","pages":[451]}]},{"heading":"Limitations","items":[{"text":"By contrast, large and dispersed markets usually require the use of intermediaries as part of the channels of distribution.","pages":[450]},{"text":"However, retailers and distributors may have better access to customers, especially in foreign markets where local knowledge of cultural norms can be an advantage.","pages":[450]}]}]},
  "Retail distribution (one intermediary)": {"d":[{"text":"A one-level distribution channel has one intermediary, such as retailers, agents or distributors being used to sell a manufacturer's products to consumers.","pages":[452]},{"text":"Retailers are the sellers of products to consumers in outlets (or 'shops').","pages":[452]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Retailers play an important role in the distribution of most products as retailers have the ability to reach large numbers of consumers, especially those that have a global reach.","pages":[446]},{"text":"However, retailers and distributors may have better access to customers, especially in foreign markets where local knowledge of cultural norms can be an advantage.","pages":[450]}]},{"heading":"Limitations for the producer","items":[{"text":"The distribution decision requires management skills in persuading retailers to stock a firm's products. Retailers such as supermarkets have limited shelf and floor space so will only want to hold supplies of products that sell well.","pages":[444]},{"text":"Most retailers rely on stocking well-known brands to attract customers. They base this decision on sales (or profits) per square metre.","pages":[447]}]}]},
  "Wholesale distribution (two intermediaries)": {"d":[{"text":"A two-level distribution channel uses two intermediaries, such as the use of wholesalers and retailers to get products to consumers.","pages":[452]},{"text":"Wholesalers are businesses that buy large quantities of a product from a manufacturer and then 'break' the bulk into smaller units for resale, mainly to retailers.","pages":[452]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages for producers and retailers","items":[{"text":"Wholesalers bear the costs of storage, thereby freeing up space for retailers, intermediaries and manufacturers.","pages":[445]},{"text":"By breaking bulk, wholesalers sell smaller batches of products to retailers and intermediaries. This helps to eliminate their need to purchase large quantities directly from a manufacturer.","pages":[445]},{"text":"There are lower transactions costs (such as invoicing and transportation) for the manufacturer, as wholesalers are the customers, not the countless smaller individual retailers and intermediaries.","pages":[445]},{"text":"Frees up time for manufacturers to focus on production, as wholesalers deal with distribution issues and problems.","pages":[445]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantage for the producer","items":[{"text":"However, a key limitation of using wholesalers is that the producer takes a risk in passing on the responsibility of marketing its products. Wholesalers might not promote the manufacturer's products in a way that it desires, thereby ruining the producer's efforts.","pages":[445]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantage for the wholesaler","items":[{"text":"Also, some larger retailers (such as hypermarkets) do not use wholesalers, choosing instead to order directly from manufacturers. This cuts out the costs of using an intermediary to the detriment of the wholesaler.","pages":[445]}]}]},
  "Specialist distributors": {"d":[{"text":"Distributors are independent and specialist businesses that trade in the products of only a few manufacturers.","pages":[446]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Book distributors usually sell the books and other publications from a limited range of publishers. This helps to ensure the distributors know the products well, as they are representing the manufacturers in the distribution process of the marketing mix.","pages":[446]},{"text":"However, retailers and distributors may have better access to customers, especially in foreign markets where local knowledge of cultural norms can be an advantage.","pages":[450]}]}]},
  "Agents and brokers": {"d":[{"text":"Agents (or brokers) are representatives and negotiators who help to sell a vendor's products, such as real estate agents or insurance brokers.","pages":[452]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Agents usually offer a range of products for consumers to choose from, perhaps from several different suppliers. For example, insurance brokers often try to find the 'best deal' for their clients from the various insurance companies that they represent.","pages":[446]},{"text":"Personal selling allows the sales agents to demonstrate how the product actually works and enables customers to ask questions relating to the product.","pages":[446]}]},{"heading":"Commission","items":[{"text":"They are experts in their particular markets and charge commission for their services (a fee based on the level or percentage of the sales made).","pages":[446]}]}]},
  "Multichannel distribution": {"d":[{"text":"A multi-channel distribution strategy refers to the use of a range of channels to distribute a firm's products.","pages":[452]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"For example, an airline company might use travel agencies, the Internet (e-commerce platforms) and airport outlets to sell its tickets. This enables the business to reach a wider range of customers, located in different areas and in different market segments.","pages":[449]}]}]},
  "E-commerce as a distribution channel": {"d":[{"text":"E-commerce is trading via the Internet.","pages":[448]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"More and more businesses now use dedicated websites to provide product information and payment options to entice customers from around the world to buy their products, from the convenience of their home or office.","pages":[448]},{"text":"It is also an effective way to reduce the costs and risks of international marketing (see Chapter 34).","pages":[448]},{"text":"The growing popularity of e-commerce means that customers are more willing to use the Internet as a distribution channel, especially with improved online payment security and the convenience of apps on mobile devices.","pages":[448]},{"text":"Speciality distribution channels can reach potential customers who do not have easy access to retail outlets.","pages":[448]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, not all products are suitable for online distribution. Customers buying cars, glasses (prescriptive spectacles) or jewellery will probably want to have direct contact with salespeople who can give a more personalised service.","pages":[448]},{"text":"Whilst e-commerce can be a convenient channel of distribution, there is a time lag between customers paying for a product and receiving it. Hence, this method is unsuitable for buying items that require urgent delivery.","pages":[450]}]}]},
  "Vending machines": {"d":[{"text":"Vending machines are specialist machines that stock products for sale, such as cigarettes, drinks, snacks, toys and even hot meals.","pages":[448]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Due to their compact size, they can be placed almost anywhere, such as an office, recreation centre, shopping mall, public car park, school, hospital or airport.","pages":[448]},{"text":"Modern vending machines allow customers to pay by a range of smartcard methods, including credit cards and debit cards, thereby enhancing their convenience.","pages":[448,449]},{"text":"A key advantage of vending machines is that running costs and maintenance costs are minimal. For example, salespeople are not needed to sell the products.","pages":[449]},{"text":"In addition, if the manufacturer owns the vending machines, it will stock only its own products which can help to reduce competition.","pages":[449]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, vending machines can be prone to vandalism and mechanical failures, which will halt sales.","pages":[449]},{"text":"Due to the low capacity of any single vending machine, only a small range of products can be sold via this distribution channel.","pages":[449]}]}]},
  "Mail order": {"d":[{"text":"Mail order is a speciality channel of distribution that enables customers to order products via the postal system.","pages":[452]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"The convenience of being able to order from home helps to entice customers to purchase a firm's products.","pages":[449]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"However, the cost of producing comprehensive and up-to-date catalogues is high, yet the shelf life is relatively short due to changing prices and new products being added to the market.","pages":[449]}]}]},
  "People and the customer experience": {"d":[{"text":"People are the employees who interact with customers, thereby delivering the service to customers.","pages":[456]}],"lists":[{"heading":"How employee service is assessed","items":[{"text":"Customers create an image of the organization in their minds based on the physical appearance as well as the body language of employees. For example, uniforms and formal business attire are worn in many occupations to portray a more professional look.","pages":[454],"point":"Appearance and body language"},{"text":"Managers will be interested in measuring the capabilities and conduct of their employees, such as whether staff have sufficient product knowledge, whether they are proactive (meaning that they are attentive to the needs of their clients) and whether they are caring, courteous and confident. If the answers to these questions are positive, then it is highly likely that customers will be happy with the service being delivered.","pages":[454],"point":"Aptitudes and attitudes"},{"text":"Comments made by various stakeholder groups can also provide useful information regarding the effectiveness of employees in delivering good customer service. Methods such as 'Employee of the month', suggestion schemes and records of customer complaints can also provide useful feedback to further improve the marketing of services and customer relations management.","pages":[454],"point":"Feedback"},{"text":"Employees who do not keep customers waiting and who do not make careless mistakes will help the business to gain a better reputation and improved corporate image.","pages":[454],"point":"Efficiency"}]},{"heading":"Challenges of managing a service","items":[{"text":"It can be relatively easy to correct mistakes made during the manufacturing of physical goods, albeit costly perhaps. However, with the provision of services it can be very difficult to correct any mistakes made. A poor corporate image caused by substandard customer service might be irreversible.","pages":[455],"point":"Correcting mistakes"},{"text":"How can we measure the output of teachers, social care workers, volunteers, news presenters, doctors and actors? They all provide a service although it can be difficult to measure their productivity in the same way as measuring the output of workers who manufacture physical products.","pages":[455,456],"point":"Measuring productivity"},{"text":"The provision of services tends to be labour-intensive, which can be costly to an organization. Lawyers, architects and accountants, for example, are paid well for their skills and expertise.","pages":[456],"point":"People management"}]}]},
  "Cultural variation in service": {"d":[{"text":"In addition, cultural variations have a direct impact on how people interact with customers.","pages":[455]}],"kind":"Textbook passage","lists":[{"heading":"Examples of cultural variation","items":[{"text":"Dominant cultures in India and Japan are averse to critical disagreements, so saying 'no' is regarded as being rather rude. Instead, any disagreements should be expressed with the use of diplomatic language.","pages":[455]},{"text":"In some cultures, staring is considered to be rude and a lack of respect, so any interaction with customers may involve minimal eye contact to give customers space (or privacy). However, in other cultures the lack of eye contact is thought to be disrespectful and suggests that a person is not paying any attention.","pages":[455]},{"text":"In the USA, it is commonly observed that supermarket staff help customers to pack their bags, whereas in Germany customers prefer to pack their own groceries.","pages":[455]},{"text":"It is common in the UK for customer service personnel to be formal and conservative, whereas the French, Italian and Spanish are rather more relaxed in their approach.","pages":[455]}]}]},
  "Service delivery processes": {"d":[{"text":"Processes are the ways in which a service is provided or delivered, including payment systems, queuing times, after-sales care and delivery services.","pages":[460]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Processes in the marketing of services","items":[{"text":"Businesses may offer their customers the convenience of different methods of payment for the purchase of goods and services, including cash, cheque, bank transfer, hire purchase (see Chapter 15) or credit card. This makes the process of paying for a service more convenient for customers, but some businesses add a surcharge for the use of credit cards or bank transfers.","pages":[457],"point":"Payment methods"},{"text":"This refers to the average length of time it takes for customers to be served, such as how long they have to wait in a queue to get into a restaurant or the time between customers placing their online orders and them receiving the goods. There can be negative consequences if businesses do not manage the waiting times for their customers, including customer complaints, disgruntled customers and a poorer corporate image.","pages":[457,458],"point":"Waiting time"},{"text":"This refers to the degree of attentiveness, care and politeness of staff towards their customers in the delivery of a good or service. The business can obtain constructive feedback on the quality of customer service from customer service satisfaction surveys, customer suggestion schemes or comment cards.","pages":[458],"point":"Customer care"},{"text":"A business can gain a competitive edge if it provides after-sales services. These are services that are offered following the sale of the product, such as free installation, maintenance services, technical support and warranties (guarantees).","pages":[458],"point":"After-sales care"},{"text":"This refers to the means of transporting goods from a source location, such as a restaurant, to a customer in another location, such as their home or place of work. Businesses can enhance the buying process of services (and goods) by providing various delivery options that are convenient for customers.","pages":[458],"point":"Delivery processes (or delivery services)"}]},{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Processes are important as they can influence the overall quality of service experienced by customers. This can have a profound impact on customer loyalty.","pages":[458]},{"text":"Providing a delivery service, whether it is free of charge or not, can give a business a competitive edge.","pages":[458]},{"text":"For example, if customers experience excellent delivery of customer care at a spa or hair salon, they are likely to tell their close friends and family, perhaps spreading the message on their social media platforms. This can provide the business with free but important viral marketing opportunities.","pages":[458]}]}]},
  "Changing a service process": {"d":[{"text":"Technological changes and the widespread adoption of smartphones and other mobile devices such as tablets have created many opportunities for businesses to improve their processes in the marketing mix. For example, many restaurants use mobile technologies for bookings and reservations, thereby improving the convenience for their customers.","pages":[459]}],"kind":"Textbook passage","lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Technological changes also create opportunities for businesses to be creative in their processes in order to engage with customers (see Question 32.1). For example, QR codes enable businesses to market their services by interacting with customers using their own mobile devices.","pages":[459]}]},{"heading":"Advantages of a checkout-free supermarket","items":[{"text":"Many customers like the convenience of a checkout-free supermarket, being able to process their purchases quickly and easily, noting the difference between traditional supermarkets and queues at the checkouts.","pages":[459]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages of a checkout-free supermarket","items":[{"text":"Other customers are more apprehensive and somewhat confused about how all the technology works and are afraid of being accused of stealing if there are technological faults or unintentional human errors.","pages":[459]},{"text":"Critics have argued that the change also discriminates against elderly people and low-income families who may not own a smartphone or know how the technology works.","pages":[459]},{"text":"Some customers complained about being unable to use the store as they do not have the storage space on their phones to download the Aldi app.","pages":[459]}]}]},
  "Physical evidence": {"d":[{"text":"Physical evidence refers to the tangible aspects of a service, such as a luxury hotel with its nice décor, spas, gyms, and swimming pools.","pages":[464]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"The physical environment is designed to make customers feel welcome, safe and comfortable in order to ensure they have a memorable experience.","pages":[462]},{"text":"Multinational retailers with stores across the world, such as McDonald's or Starbucks, focus on creating hygienic, homogeneous and hospitable environments. This helps to reassure people who like a sense of familiarity and tangible security, wherever they might be in the world.","pages":[461]},{"text":"Guests are very likely to consider the physical environment before choosing between competing hotels. Hence, creative marketing strategies that focus on the hotel's physical evidence can help to give the business a competitive advantage.","pages":[464]},{"text":"It is worth noting that the physical environment is also important in marketing an organization to existing and potential employees. People are more likely to be productive, motivated and loyal to organizations that consider the importance of the physical working environment.","pages":[464]}]},{"heading":"Limitations","items":[{"text":"However, physical evidence is only one of 7 components of the marketing mix. In summary, all aspects of the marketing mix need to be considered in the context of the product and the changing needs and preferences of customers and other key stakeholder groups.","pages":[462,464]}]}]},
  "International marketing": {"d":[{"text":"International marketing is the marketing of an organization's goods and services in overseas countries.","pages":[473]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Opportunities","items":[{"text":"The size of a market can be enlarged by marketing products to overseas buyers. This should lead to greater sales revenue and possibly higher market share for the business.","pages":[465],"point":"Increased customer base"},{"text":"By operating on a larger and international scale, a business is likely to benefit from cost savings known as economies of scale (see Chapter 5). These cost-reducing benefits of larger scale operations can enable growing businesses to gain higher profit margins and/or to reduce their prices (thereby giving them a price advantage over their competitors).","pages":[465,466],"point":"Economies of scale"},{"text":"Having a standardized marketing strategy across the world (such as using identical packaging and advertising for global brands) not only reduces average costs of production but can also lead to greater international recognition of a brand. This can lead to improved brand loyalty and increased global sales revenues.","pages":[466],"point":"Increase brand recognition"},{"text":"By operating in various international markets, a business is less exposed to the risks in one particular country (such as a recession or changes in fashion and tastes).","pages":[466],"point":"Spread risks"},{"text":"Having a wider distribution channel (see Chapter 30) increases the potential number of foreign buyers. For example, Japanese car producers Honda, Toyota and Nissan all have manufacturing plants in France and the UK, enabling these producers to gain access to the huge markets in Europe markets.","pages":[466],"point":"Wider distribution channels"},{"text":"A business might find that the domestic market for its product is saturated or in decline. By marketing the product overseas, the business can expand its life cycle to generate higher sales revenues.","pages":[466],"point":"Extend the product life cycle"},{"text":"Ultimately, all the reasons above for international marketing can help to generate more profits for the business. Overseas markets provide a significantly larger source of revenue so can be financially lucrative.","pages":[466],"point":"Gain more profit"}]},{"heading":"Threats · legal issues","items":[{"text":"Entry into international markets can be a problem due to different legal systems. For example, cigarette advertising is still permissible in several European countries, but advertising tobacco products on television has been banned in the USA since 1971.","pages":[467]},{"text":"Even the same law (such as packaging and labelling regulation on food products) may have different standards in different countries. This means it might be more difficult (and hence more expensive) to sell the same products in overseas markets.","pages":[467]},{"text":"Beyond legislation, governments can also set up other international trade barriers to protect their domestic industries (see Box 34.1), thereby creating a threat to businesses trying to enter and operate in overseas markets.","pages":[467]},{"text":"Intellectual property rights (see Units 17 and 42) must also be adhered to. This covers copyright, trademark and patent legislations, including matters such as brand names, slogans, inventions, creative works of art and processes already legally assigned to other businesses.","pages":[467]},{"text":"Differences in consumer protection laws must also be observed by international marketers. Many countries have their own code of conduct on advertising and packaging information, for example, so these must be respected.","pages":[467]}]},{"heading":"Threats · political issues","items":[{"text":"The degree of political stability within a country can have a major impact on whether international marketers succeed in overseas markets. For example, trade protectionist measures (see Box 34.1) such as tariffs, quotas and subsidies for domestic businesses, create major obstacles for international marketing.","pages":[467]},{"text":"Political and social unrest, which can sometimes spark violent protests, as well as corruption, will destabilize operational and strategic decisions for international marketers operating in these countries.","pages":[467]}]},{"heading":"Threats · social and demographic issues","items":[{"text":"Different socio-economic and demographic conditions mean that businesses may need to reconsider aspects of their international marketing. For example, Samsung's product range and prices for its smartphones will differ when marketing to customers in low-income countries compared with the products and prices for its smartphones targeted at customers in high-income nations.","pages":[468]},{"text":"Demographics is the study of population trends and it is important for marketers to recognise that demographic changes will have direct impacts on international marketing.","pages":[468]},{"text":"Similarly, cultural norms of the population need to be considered. For example, frozen food products such as microwave-ready meals are popular in the US and many European countries but are not in most Southeast Asian nations.","pages":[468]},{"text":"Language is another issue that needs to be considered. Overseas customers in Japan (expatriates and tourists) are not very well catered for as only about 1% of the population are foreigners.","pages":[468]},{"text":"Organizations also need to consider societal norms regarding business etiquette. This refers to the mannerisms and customs by which business is conducted in different parts of the world.","pages":[468]},{"text":"A final social factor the international marketers need to consider is the potential threat of pressure groups. Pressure groups (see chapter 4) are concerned with the impacts of business activity on society, so these organisations can also create problems for marketers hoping to gain a foothold in foreign markets.","pages":[469]}]},{"heading":"Threats · economic issues","items":[{"text":"However, the main threat in this case is the high costs of overseas investment, which can harm the cash flow and profits of the business in the short term.","pages":[469]},{"text":"However, international marketing of products also increases the degree of competition in the marketplace. Transportation costs, exchange rate fluctuations, interest rates and communication costs are further economic issues that need to be considered when marketing products overseas.","pages":[470]},{"text":"Furthermore, marketers need to consider the stability of the economy as this directly affects the degree of success of international marketing. During a global financial crisis, for example, most businesses will struggle irrespective of their marketing strategies, experience and budgets.","pages":[470]}]}]},
  "Market standardisation": {"d":[{"text":"Some products have become so popular on an international scale that they are called global brands, i.e. the same product is sold with exactly the same (or very similar) marketing strategies used in overseas markets.","pages":[407]}],"kind":"Textbook passage","lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Having a standardized marketing strategy across the world (such as using identical packaging and advertising for global brands) not only reduces average costs of production but can also lead to greater international recognition of a brand. This can lead to improved brand loyalty and increased global sales revenues.","pages":[466],"point":"Increase brand recognition"},{"text":"The key driving forces for global branding strategies are brand exposure and the cost savings gained from using a single international marketing approach.","pages":[407]}]},{"heading":"Limitations","items":[{"text":"The dilemma facing marketing managers is trying to gain economies of scale by using a single international marketing strategy yet still cater for local or regional preferences and tastes.","pages":[470]},{"text":"Nevertheless, ethical and cultural considerations have led to a greater focus on the local needs of overseas customers. Thus, many global brands have been glocalised to cater for local preferences whilst retaining the core elements of the global brand.","pages":[407]}]}]},
  "Local adaptation and glocalisation": {"d":[{"text":"Thus, many global brands have been glocalised to cater for local preferences whilst retaining the core elements of the global brand. For example, the core food menu at McDonald's is almost the same throughout the world, but different products are also included to cater for local tastes and preferences.","pages":[407]}],"kind":"Textbook passage","lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"Despite the uniformity and opportunities brought by globalization and international marketing strategies, countries still have their national identities through language, culture, and other unique characteristics. Hence, marketing strategies are likely to need businesses to adapt products and services to meet the varying needs of their international clients.","pages":[471]},{"text":"An understanding and awareness of international business etiquette can ultimately give an organization a competitive advantage in an ever-competitive and changing business world.","pages":[471]}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"After all, having to change products to suit local preferences or cultures can be costly.","pages":[470]}]}]},
  "Exporting through a distributor": {"d":[{"text":"Distributors are independent and specialist businesses that trade in the products of only a few manufacturers.","pages":[446]},{"text":"The globalization of markets and increase in international trade have been fuelled by the rising number of businesses that sell their products in overseas markets via export markets and e-commerce.","pages":[451]}],"kind":"Textbook passages","lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"This helps to ensure the distributors know the products well, as they are representing the manufacturers in the distribution process of the marketing mix.","pages":[446]},{"text":"Having a wider distribution channel (see Chapter 30) increases the potential number of foreign buyers.","pages":[466],"point":"Wider distribution channels"}]},{"heading":"Constraints on exporting","items":[{"text":"The changing nature of exchange rates can make products cheaper (in which case export sales are likely to rise) but can also lead to higher export prices (in which case, demand will most probably fall). This creates uncertainty for businesses distributing their products in foreign markets.","pages":[451],"point":"Fluctuating exchanges rates"},{"text":"Diversity in cultural norms might mean that some products are not suited to certain regions or countries, such as pork products in Muslim countries. Similarly, language barriers could exist.","pages":[451],"point":"Cultural differences"},{"text":"Overseas governments may place trade barriers and legal constraints on exported products (see Box 34.1). Businesses also need to be aware of the changes to government rules and regulations that take place from time to time.","pages":[451],"point":"Government intervention"}]}]},
  "Local joint venture": {"d":[{"text":"A joint venture (JV) occurs when two or more businesses split the costs, risks, control and rewards of a project. In doing so, the parties agree to set up a new legal business entity.","pages":[80]},{"text":"Therefore, many businesses choose to use joint ventures and strategic alliances with local partners, which has proven to be highly practical for international marketers.","pages":[471]}],"lists":[{"heading":"Advantages","items":[{"text":"The pooling of experiences, skills and resources of the collaborating firms in the JV should create synergies.","pages":[80],"point":"Synergy"},{"text":"Financial costs, risks and losses are shared in a JV thereby helping to reduce the financial burden on any single organization. JVs also allow firms to diversify their products, thereby helping to spread the risks associated with growth and evolution.","pages":[81],"point":"Spreading of costs and risks"},{"text":"Joint ventures are used by companies to enter foreign countries by forming an agreement with local firms. In some cases, national laws make JVs the only option for businesses wishing to enter foreign markets.","pages":[81],"point":"Entry to foreign markets"},{"text":"Mergers and acquisitions can be highly expensive as they entail lengthy legal procedures and administrative costs. By contrast, JVs are cheaper to establish and are easier to pull out of should this be necessary.","pages":[81],"point":"Relatively cheap"},{"text":"Competition is reduced by forming a JV. Companies cooperating in a JV are unlikely to directly compete with each other, yet their pooled resources make them a stronger force against their rivals.","pages":[81],"point":"Competitive advantages"},{"text":"Firms that expand via international JVs can take advantage of each other's local knowledge and reputation. This might not be the case with mergers or acquisitions that are exposed to potential problems of overseas expansion (such as differences in business etiquette, cultural values, languages and local traditions).","pages":[81],"point":"Exploitation of local knowledge"},{"text":"Joint ventures tend to be friendly and well received by key stakeholder groups. The parties pool their funds and resources, sharing responsibility for their mutual benefit.","pages":[81],"point":"High success rate"}]},{"heading":"Disadvantages","items":[{"text":"Partners in a JV have to rely heavily on the resources and goodwill of their counterparts.","pages":[81]},{"text":"There is also likely to be a dilution of the brands, yet firms spend huge amounts of money trying to develop their own brands (see Chapter 27).","pages":[81]},{"text":"Finally, whenever companies work together on a project, there is always the possibility of organizational culture clashes that can lead to operational problems and conflicts for the joint venture.","pages":[81]}]}]},
  "Exchange-rate exposure": {"d":[{"text":"The exchange rate measures the value of the domestic currency in terms of foreign currencies.","pages":[626]},{"text":"Continual and large fluctuations in exchange rates can create threats for businesses. Business planning and forecasting become very complex and perhaps impractical.","pages":[626]}],"kind":"Textbook passages","lists":[{"heading":"Exchange-rate appreciation","items":[{"text":"A higher exchange rate (known as an appreciation of the currency) means that export prices will be relatively higher, thereby reducing the exporter's price competitiveness.","pages":[626]}]},{"heading":"Exchange-rate depreciation","items":[{"text":"Although a lower exchange rate (known as a depreciation of the currency) should give domestic firms a relative price advantage, it also means that they have to pay higher prices for imported raw materials and components.","pages":[626]},{"text":"Predicting the effects of exchange rate fluctuations, e.g., exporters will generally benefit from lower exchange rates as their prices become more competitive (so long as the PED for exports is price elastic).","pages":[418]}]},{"heading":"Uncertainty and forecasting","items":[{"text":"Business planning and forecasting become very complex and perhaps impractical. For example, businesses may not be able to accurately forecast export sales or costs of imported materials due to exchange rate volatility.","pages":[626]},{"text":"International trade deals could be postponed until the business can benefit from more favourable movements in the exchange rate (although there is no guarantee that this will ever happen).","pages":[626]}]}]},
  "Barriers to market entry": {"d":[{"text":"Beyond legislation, governments can also set up other international trade barriers to protect their domestic industries (see Box 34.1), thereby creating a threat to businesses trying to enter and operate in overseas markets.","pages":[467]}],"kind":"Textbook passage","lists":[{"heading":"International trade barriers","items":[{"text":"These are import taxes, which increase the price of imports (thereby supporting domestic firms) as well as helping to raise government revenues.","pages":[467],"point":"Tariffs"},{"text":"These are quantitative restrictions on imported goods, thus limiting the number of foreign products allowed to enter the country.","pages":[467],"point":"Quotas"},{"text":"These are outright bans on certain products entering a country due to major health and safety threats or political conflict, such as the US trade embargoes imposed on Cuba since 1962.","pages":[467],"point":"Embargoes"},{"text":"These are bureaucratic obstacles such as safety regulations, licenses and employment visas (thereby making entry much more difficult).","pages":[467],"point":"Administrative barriers"},{"text":"These are a form of financial assistance given by the domestic government to domestic businesses in order to reduce their costs of production (hence giving them a cost advantage over foreign competitors).","pages":[467],"point":"Subsidies"}]}]}
};
